WEBVTT
Kind: captions

00:00:54.488 --> 00:00:55.881 position:63%
[music]

00:00:59.726 --> 00:01:02.929 position:63%
[Peter] Kirk, we have the symbol
of the atom up there.

00:01:02.996 --> 00:01:06.212 position:63%
And this started
because of the Manhattan Project, right?

00:01:06.279 --> 00:01:08.599 position:63%
[Kirk] Right.
This is Richland, Washington.

00:01:08.666 --> 00:01:09.936 position:63%
We're a nuclear town.

00:01:10.003 --> 00:01:12.272 position:63%
We're at the entrance to the Hanford site.

00:01:12.339 --> 00:01:14.641 position:63%
That's since 1943.

00:01:14.708 --> 00:01:20.247 position:63%
-Yeah, and so Hanford was very important
in the building of the atom bomb.

00:01:20.314 --> 00:01:23.790 position:63%
-Yes, the plutonium bomb.
-The plutonium element of it.

00:01:23.857 --> 00:01:28.321 position:63%
Yeah, because they started with
both enriched uranium and plutonium.

00:01:28.388 --> 00:01:31.825 position:63%
They found out right away, the more
bang for your buck was the plutonium.

00:01:31.892 --> 00:01:36.129 position:63%
The government built a whole secret city
out in the desert where we're going today.

00:01:36.196 --> 00:01:37.063 position:63%
Yes, yes.

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:38.705 position:63%
This is Spudnut.

00:01:38.772 --> 00:01:40.600 position:63%
This is a little institution here.

00:01:40.667 --> 00:01:42.334 position:63%
It's been around forever.

00:01:43.287 --> 00:01:44.871 position:63%
-[woman] Thank you.
-Yeah. Mm-hmm.

00:01:44.938 --> 00:01:46.667 position:63%
[diners chattering]

00:01:47.207 --> 00:01:48.708 position:63%
[Kirk] Hi there, how are you?

00:01:48.775 --> 00:01:52.178 position:63%
I'd like to introduce you to Peter.
He's a YouTuber.

00:01:52.245 --> 00:01:54.547 position:63%
And if you get a chance,
you should go look at his channel

00:01:54.614 --> 00:01:56.116 position:63%
because it's a lot of good content.

00:01:56.183 --> 00:01:59.152 position:63%
We're on our way out to
the Hanford site right now.

00:01:59.219 --> 00:02:00.501 position:63%
I'm gonna show him the site.

00:02:00.568 --> 00:02:02.656 position:63%
I worked out there for 30 years.

00:02:02.723 --> 00:02:03.790 position:63%
I got you by 10.

00:02:04.270 --> 00:02:06.919 position:63%
-Okay, how was it?
-How was it?

00:02:06.986 --> 00:02:09.248 position:63%
-Yeah, how was that experience?
-Did you see the glow?

00:02:09.321 --> 00:02:10.690 position:63%
-Yeah.
-[chuckling]

00:02:10.757 --> 00:02:11.924 position:63%
He's still got his hair.

00:02:12.972 --> 00:02:14.972 position:63%
-It really was up and down.

00:02:15.519 --> 00:02:16.352 position:63%
[laughter]

00:02:17.396 --> 00:02:18.938 position:63%
Politically, a roller coaster.

00:02:19.005 --> 00:02:22.488 position:63%
I was a chief architect
for the Vit Plant out there.

00:02:22.555 --> 00:02:23.388 position:63%
-Okay.

00:02:23.455 --> 00:02:25.870 position:63%
And I just retired in 2016.

00:02:26.446 --> 00:02:30.642 position:63%
So I got to see the start of
the new glass facility that's there now.

00:02:30.709 --> 00:02:34.020 position:63%
-You're taking the nuclear material,
turning it into glass?

00:02:34.087 --> 00:02:35.422 position:63%
You were in Los Alamos?

00:02:35.489 --> 00:02:39.593 position:63%
Special teams for DOE
to go visit other sites.

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:40.927 position:63%
-What were you doing out there?

00:02:40.994 --> 00:02:42.767 position:63%
Fire protection engineer.

00:02:42.834 --> 00:02:46.232 position:63%
-Big difference between the areas?
Los Alamos and here?

00:02:46.299 --> 00:02:47.499 position:63%
-No?
-All the same.

00:02:48.301 --> 00:02:49.135 position:63%
[Kirk] See ya.

00:02:49.836 --> 00:02:51.503 position:63%
[Peter] Thanks, you guys.

00:02:52.873 --> 00:02:56.943 position:63%
[Peter] So Kirk,
we got some unique special access today.

00:02:57.010 --> 00:02:58.346 position:63%
-Oh, definitely. Yeah, right.

00:02:58.413 --> 00:03:01.514 position:63%
[Peter] The nuclear facilities
are out in the desert.

00:03:01.581 --> 00:03:03.283 position:63%
-Yes, 25 miles.

00:03:03.350 --> 00:03:05.852 position:63%
This is considered a special tour today.

00:03:05.919 --> 00:03:08.656 position:63%
-Okay, so this was all built
in the 50s, right?

00:03:08.723 --> 00:03:11.758 position:63%
-This was the model town.
-Late 40s, early 50s.

00:03:11.825 --> 00:03:14.427 position:63%
It's definitely atomic age, retro.

00:03:14.494 --> 00:03:17.297 position:63%
-And built almost overnight?
-Oh, everything was.

00:03:17.364 --> 00:03:20.567 position:63%
The houses came in as prefab
and they'd set them up.

00:03:20.634 --> 00:03:24.404 position:63%
And the people who lived in the houses
were not responsible for anything.

00:03:24.471 --> 00:03:26.018 position:63%
They didn't even change light bulbs.

00:03:26.085 --> 00:03:28.374 position:63%
So the government took care of everything.

00:03:28.441 --> 00:03:32.879 position:63%
When I got here in '79, they still had
buses running around picking up people

00:03:32.946 --> 00:03:37.550 position:63%
in Richland to take them out to a bus lot,
to take them out to where their jobs were.

00:03:37.617 --> 00:03:40.152 position:63%
Lots of times people didn't even know
what their jobs were

00:03:40.219 --> 00:03:41.821 position:63%
because it was top secret.

00:03:41.888 --> 00:03:43.556 position:63%
Before I was able to go to work out here,

00:03:43.623 --> 00:03:45.703 position:63%
I had to get a Q clearance.

00:03:45.770 --> 00:03:47.494 position:63%
Which is the top secret clearance.

00:03:48.128 --> 00:03:52.033 position:63%
When they started the production
of the bombs, producing plutonium,

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:54.234 position:63%
they made a witch's brew.

00:03:54.301 --> 00:03:58.590 position:63%
They took irradiated uranium,
super radioactive,

00:03:59.107 --> 00:04:03.677 position:63%
mixed it with acids and that
to pull the plutonium element out.

00:04:03.744 --> 00:04:06.099 position:63%
So then you had a lot of leftovers.

00:04:06.166 --> 00:04:09.049 position:63%
It was just nasty and that's the problem.

00:04:11.819 --> 00:04:15.019 position:63%
[Peter] Manhattan Project
National Historic Park.

00:04:15.488 --> 00:04:19.880 position:63%
So there were three main places
for the Manhattan Project, Los Alamos.

00:04:19.947 --> 00:04:21.928 position:63%
-That's where they assembled the weapons.
-Yep.

00:04:21.995 --> 00:04:25.733 position:63%
Oak Ridge, Tennessee
is where they produce enriched uranium

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:27.644 position:63%
and here was the plutonium.

00:04:27.711 --> 00:04:32.440 position:63%
And plutonium became the element of choice
for the atomic weapons, nuclear weapons.

00:04:32.507 --> 00:04:35.722 position:63%
-And it was Fat Man
that was developed out of here

00:04:35.789 --> 00:04:37.777 position:63%
that was dropped on Nagasaki, right?

00:04:37.844 --> 00:04:38.978 position:63%
Yes.

00:04:39.813 --> 00:04:42.615 position:63%
-Can we show some of these pictures?
-Oh, all this. This is public.

00:04:42.682 --> 00:04:45.483 position:63%
This is a picture of
the construction camp.

00:04:45.550 --> 00:04:47.887 position:63%
There was 55,000 folks working

00:04:47.954 --> 00:04:50.143 position:63%
to build the B, D, and F reactor.

00:04:50.210 --> 00:04:52.659 position:63%
Those were the Manhattan Project reactors.

00:04:52.726 --> 00:04:56.142 position:63%
55,000 segregated by sex and race.

00:04:56.729 --> 00:05:01.033 position:63%
So even if there was a married couple,
the females would be in the…

00:05:01.101 --> 00:05:02.669 position:63%
-Oh, with the housing?
-Yes.

00:05:02.736 --> 00:05:04.827 position:63%
-And these are all the workers
that were building it?

00:05:04.894 --> 00:05:06.410 position:63%
-Yes.
-What is this out here?

00:05:06.477 --> 00:05:10.323 position:63%
-Well, they were losing people because
they were missing their families…

00:05:10.390 --> 00:05:12.545 position:63%
So they allowed them
to have trailer village.

00:05:12.612 --> 00:05:14.882 position:63%
They allowed them to move into these.

00:05:14.949 --> 00:05:15.803 position:63%
-Okay.

00:05:15.870 --> 00:05:20.687 position:63%
But see, you got to remember
construction was only about 20 months.

00:05:20.754 --> 00:05:22.756 position:63%
-So people from all over
the country came out here?

00:05:22.823 --> 00:05:25.091 position:63%
-Yeah. You see where the majority
of the folks come from.

00:05:25.158 --> 00:05:26.694 position:63%
It was really the South.

00:05:26.761 --> 00:05:30.030 position:63%
-Okay. So like Great Migration?
-Oh, yeah. Definitely.

00:05:30.097 --> 00:05:31.899 position:63%
-But to Washington instead.
-To Washington.

00:05:31.966 --> 00:05:35.268 position:63%
-And we're like right here, right?
In Washington, out there?

00:05:35.335 --> 00:05:38.519 position:63%
-There was 8 mess halls
that fed 5,000 people

00:05:38.586 --> 00:05:40.573 position:63%
for breakfast, lunch and dinner.

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:45.046 position:63%
[Peter] You had an auditorium, could hold
4,000 dancers, theaters, billiards.

00:05:45.113 --> 00:05:48.014 position:63%
-Well, they had to give the people
something to do when they're not working.

00:05:48.081 --> 00:05:50.617 position:63%
They had it all set up with entertainment.

00:05:50.684 --> 00:05:53.686 position:63%
Ronald Reagan was here.
I think John Wayne was here.

00:05:53.753 --> 00:05:55.056 position:63%
They brought all those folks through.

00:05:55.123 --> 00:05:58.057 position:63%
-And then the workers
coming out to build this…

00:05:58.124 --> 00:06:00.752 position:63%
-They didn't know what they were doing.
-Not a clue.

00:06:00.819 --> 00:06:03.338 position:63%
-And so they're bringing their families
into this environment.

00:06:03.405 --> 00:06:06.699 position:63%
Well, now, these folks were here before.
This is pre-Manhattan.

00:06:06.766 --> 00:06:09.503 position:63%
The community pictures.
This is White Bluffs.

00:06:09.570 --> 00:06:11.874 position:63%
-Okay, so it was like that.
-Oh, it was beautiful.

00:06:11.941 --> 00:06:14.741 position:63%
-Really Wild West stuff.
-Yeah, right. Yeah.

00:06:14.808 --> 00:06:17.443 position:63%
These folks were given 30 days
to leave their property

00:06:17.510 --> 00:06:18.812 position:63%
when the government came here.

00:06:18.879 --> 00:06:21.848 position:63%
-The government bought
thousands of plots of land, right?

00:06:21.915 --> 00:06:24.017 position:63%
They gave them
what they said the value was.

00:06:24.084 --> 00:06:26.653 position:63%
And lots of times, that's disputed, right?

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:28.888 position:63%
So it was for national security.
That was the argument?

00:06:28.955 --> 00:06:31.826 position:63%
Oh, yeah, yeah. Right. Well, and safety.

00:06:31.893 --> 00:06:34.297 position:63%
Because nobody had built this reactor.

00:06:34.364 --> 00:06:35.895 position:63%
I mean, it's a production reactor,

00:06:35.962 --> 00:06:40.400 position:63%
and the only time they just proved
the theory in Chicago, Enrico Fermi,

00:06:40.467 --> 00:06:44.304 position:63%
proved the theory that they were able to
make the element plutonium,

00:06:44.371 --> 00:06:48.500 position:63%
which is a man-made element.
It is not natural in nature.

00:06:48.567 --> 00:06:49.733 position:63%
-So it was all experimental.

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:52.512 position:63%
It was the first time for this
in the history of the world.

00:06:52.579 --> 00:06:53.446 position:63%
Yes.

00:06:53.513 --> 00:06:55.756 position:63%
-And so they don't really know
how it's going to go.

00:06:55.823 --> 00:06:58.952 position:63%
No. No, and they didn't know
what was going on.

00:06:59.019 --> 00:07:01.171 position:63%
This is the front face of the B Reactor.

00:07:01.238 --> 00:07:05.057 position:63%
This is where the fuel went in.
The natural uranium.

00:07:05.124 --> 00:07:08.061 position:63%
Uranium elements were
about an inch and a half in diameter

00:07:08.128 --> 00:07:09.662 position:63%
by about eight inches long.

00:07:09.729 --> 00:07:11.396 position:63%
There's over 2,000 tubes.

00:07:11.875 --> 00:07:13.420 position:63%
It's 40 feet thick.

00:07:13.487 --> 00:07:16.629 position:63%
It's cooled by water.
They put the elements in.

00:07:17.550 --> 00:07:21.841 position:63%
They'd pull the rods out
that keep it from going critical.

00:07:21.908 --> 00:07:23.276 position:63%
But as soon as that goes critical,

00:07:23.343 --> 00:07:25.645 position:63%
they got the water in there
and that keeps it cool.

00:07:25.712 --> 00:07:29.949 position:63%
When the water goes through,
40 feet, it comes out almost boiling.

00:07:30.550 --> 00:07:31.818 position:63%
This is Hanford.

00:07:31.885 --> 00:07:35.021 position:63%
The town is pretty much gone,
except for the school building.

00:07:35.088 --> 00:07:37.490 position:63%
And there is what they call the Ice House.

00:07:37.557 --> 00:07:40.293 position:63%
-We just drove through Richland.
-Yep.

00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:41.728 position:63%
-We're down there in the Uptown.

00:07:41.795 --> 00:07:43.463 position:63%
-And so we're getting out up here, right?

00:07:43.530 --> 00:07:45.463 position:63%
-Yeah. We're headed this way.

00:07:47.901 --> 00:07:51.037 position:63%
[Peter] So this part of Washington
is a totally different Washington

00:07:51.104 --> 00:07:52.372 position:63%
than what most people know.

00:07:52.439 --> 00:07:54.040 position:63%
[Kirk] We get six inches of rain a year.

00:07:54.107 --> 00:07:57.510 position:63%
It's called shrub-steppe.
So it's just above a desert.

00:07:57.577 --> 00:07:59.913 position:63%
And the reason we're able to have
the agriculture

00:07:59.980 --> 00:08:01.514 position:63%
is because of the Columbia River.

00:08:01.581 --> 00:08:04.384 position:63%
The Yakima River and the Snake River.
They're all right here.

00:08:04.451 --> 00:08:07.253 position:63%
-So you got the water coming through,
just not falling from the sky.

00:08:07.320 --> 00:08:08.350 position:63%
-Yep. Exactly.

00:08:09.689 --> 00:08:11.391 position:63%
[Kirk] Let me get me a drink.

00:08:11.458 --> 00:08:12.525 position:63%
Ah, there we go.

00:08:13.393 --> 00:08:15.962 position:63%
-We forgot to introduce Robert.
-Robert, you're a historian.

00:08:16.029 --> 00:08:18.031 position:63%
Yes. Professor of history.

00:08:18.098 --> 00:08:19.232 position:63%
-We can't get out there yet,

00:08:19.299 --> 00:08:21.868 position:63%
but what's the basic nuts and bolts
of what's going on?

00:08:21.935 --> 00:08:23.870 position:63%
-So this is called the 200 Area.

00:08:23.937 --> 00:08:26.606 position:63%
This is the plutonium processing area,

00:08:26.673 --> 00:08:30.677 position:63%
where the Hanford site would take
irradiated fuel from the reactors,

00:08:30.744 --> 00:08:33.646 position:63%
bring it up to these long
concrete facilities here,

00:08:33.713 --> 00:08:36.349 position:63%
chemically dissolve it,
isolate the plutonium,

00:08:36.416 --> 00:08:39.886 position:63%
and then that's just a little bit of it,
less than a percent.

00:08:39.953 --> 00:08:41.020 position:63%
The rest would be waste.

00:08:41.087 --> 00:08:43.191 position:63%
And that's how we got the 56 million
gallons

00:08:43.258 --> 00:08:44.624 position:63%
of high-level nuclear waste.

00:08:44.691 --> 00:08:46.592 position:63%
-High-level waste.
-That's the high-level waste.

00:08:46.659 --> 00:08:48.027 position:63%
-That's the big cleanup operation?

00:08:48.094 --> 00:08:49.896 position:63%
This is where
the big cleanup operation is.

00:08:49.963 --> 00:08:53.566 position:63%
This is where Hanford
really did the majority of its dirty work.

00:08:53.633 --> 00:08:54.834 position:63%
-It's still doing it right now?

00:08:54.901 --> 00:08:58.438 position:63%
We're still cleaning it up
and we will be for decades.

00:08:58.505 --> 00:09:01.674 position:63%
So a lot of people are employed now,
are working out there.

00:09:01.741 --> 00:09:03.810 position:63%
-A lot of people in town.
-Yes, thousands.

00:09:03.877 --> 00:09:06.879 position:63%
More now than during
the height of the production.

00:09:06.946 --> 00:09:07.847 position:63%
-Seriously?
-Yeah.

00:09:07.914 --> 00:09:09.582 position:63%
Lots of jobs out there.

00:09:09.649 --> 00:09:14.088 position:63%
We're on the third or fourth generation
of Hanford workers right now.

00:09:14.155 --> 00:09:15.221 position:63%
Okay. Wow.

00:09:15.888 --> 00:09:18.591 position:63%
[Peter] So the cleanup crew,
people from around the country.

00:09:18.658 --> 00:09:19.990 position:63%
-[Kirk] Oh, yeah.
-[Robert] Yeah.

00:09:20.057 --> 00:09:24.038 position:63%
[Kirk] There's over $2 billion a year
that come in just to do this cleanup.

00:09:24.105 --> 00:09:27.781 position:63%
[Kirk] And that doesn't count the billion
that goes to the lab that we have here.

00:09:27.848 --> 00:09:31.020 position:63%
And we have the second largest
national lab in the lab system here.

00:09:31.087 --> 00:09:33.039 position:63%
-That's still operating?
-Oh, yeah. Oh, yeah.

00:09:33.106 --> 00:09:35.192 position:63%
The largest employer.

00:09:35.259 --> 00:09:37.576 position:63%
They started out doing Hanford stuff.

00:09:37.643 --> 00:09:41.514 position:63%
They actually started out on the site
running an animal experimentation farm.

00:09:41.581 --> 00:09:43.950 position:63%
They moved off site.
And they've expanded so much.

00:09:44.017 --> 00:09:46.434 position:63%
They still do some Hanford work
but they do…

00:09:46.501 --> 00:09:49.088 position:63%
Geez, they do work for magnets, for MRIs.

00:09:49.155 --> 00:09:52.326 position:63%
They do DOD work.
They do the cybersecurity work.

00:09:52.393 --> 00:09:55.161 position:63%
It's huge
and they keep actually expanding.

00:09:55.228 --> 00:09:58.197 position:63%
They've been expanding.
We see some of it later.

00:09:58.264 --> 00:10:00.166 position:63%
It's a massive national lab.

00:10:00.233 --> 00:10:03.636 position:63%
[Kirk] So we're gonna be
driving on some original highway.

00:10:03.703 --> 00:10:07.103 position:63%
-[Peter] All right. Special access?
-Special access.

00:10:12.879 --> 00:10:15.615 position:63%
[Peter] So we're officially
on the Hanford site.

00:10:15.682 --> 00:10:18.351 position:63%
-[Peter] Half the size of Rhode Island?
-[Robert] Correct.

00:10:18.418 --> 00:10:22.021 position:63%
Why did the US government decide here?
Just because it's so remote?

00:10:22.088 --> 00:10:24.357 position:63%
-Several reasons,
and I'll give them to you real quick.

00:10:24.424 --> 00:10:25.858 position:63%
-Yeah.
-It's remote.

00:10:25.925 --> 00:10:28.928 position:63%
There's a big natural boundary here
with all the hills.

00:10:28.995 --> 00:10:33.699 position:63%
They had to have fast-flowing cold water
to cool these reactors.

00:10:33.766 --> 00:10:36.335 position:63%
All the reactors
are along the river for that reason.

00:10:36.402 --> 00:10:40.973 position:63%
And Big Pasco, you had
two major rail lines that meet there.

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:44.544 position:63%
So you could get
everything you needed here to this region.

00:10:44.611 --> 00:10:46.370 position:63%
But then this area is remote.

00:10:46.437 --> 00:10:48.648 position:63%
So you had that transportation.

00:10:48.715 --> 00:10:52.052 position:63%
You had the river. You had electricity.
You had the remoteness.

00:10:52.119 --> 00:10:53.786 position:63%
It was basically perfect.

00:10:53.877 --> 00:10:59.625 position:63%
When Franklin Matthias flew over
this region on December 22nd, 1942,

00:10:59.692 --> 00:11:02.261 position:63%
he had been asked by General Groves
to scout locations.

00:11:02.328 --> 00:11:04.097 position:63%
He had not seen anything that quite fit.

00:11:04.164 --> 00:11:07.334 position:63%
And when he flew over this area,
he knew right away. This is it.

00:11:07.401 --> 00:11:09.469 position:63%
[Peter] This was the state highway
back in the day.

00:11:09.536 --> 00:11:10.369 position:63%
-This was.

00:11:10.436 --> 00:11:12.472 position:63%
And then we can get to where
everyone lived, right?

00:11:12.539 --> 00:11:15.108 position:63%
-Out to some of the infrastructure.
-Yes. We'll get to Hanford.

00:11:15.175 --> 00:11:16.633 position:63%
This is the imagination tour.

00:11:16.700 --> 00:11:17.819 position:63%
There's a lot gone.

00:11:17.886 --> 00:11:19.291 position:63%
So you have to use your imagination

00:11:19.358 --> 00:11:21.381 position:63%
but once we get out to
Hanford, White Bluffs,

00:11:21.448 --> 00:11:25.118 position:63%
you'll start to see trees
out in the middle of these fields.

00:11:25.185 --> 00:11:26.953 position:63%
And someone had to plant that tree.

00:11:27.020 --> 00:11:29.889 position:63%
So once we start to see
trees out by themselves,

00:11:29.956 --> 00:11:32.859 position:63%
you start to get a sense of
where people were,

00:11:32.926 --> 00:11:34.727 position:63%
where there was life.

00:11:34.794 --> 00:11:36.195 position:63%
Right? There's the pump house.

00:11:36.262 --> 00:11:38.155 position:63%
So that's Manhattan Project era.

00:11:38.222 --> 00:11:40.964 position:63%
Pumps water up, and it pumps it up.
We're driving right by it.

00:11:41.031 --> 00:11:44.670 position:63%
There's a 25 million gallon reservoir
right up here.

00:11:44.737 --> 00:11:49.842 position:63%
And this is still used
to pump water up to the 200 Area today.

00:11:49.909 --> 00:11:52.178 position:63%
There's a 200 East and a 200 West.

00:11:52.245 --> 00:11:56.282 position:63%
And both of them have
canyon process facilities and…

00:11:56.349 --> 00:11:58.284 position:63%
-Waste tanks.
-And waste tanks, right.

00:11:58.351 --> 00:12:01.120 position:63%
Which is the big…
The big thing today is those waste tanks.

00:12:01.187 --> 00:12:03.356 position:63%
-It's the waste tanks?
That's the issue right now.

00:12:03.423 --> 00:12:07.560 position:63%
That is, that is the… what the almost,
not our sole focus, but it's the big one.

00:12:07.627 --> 00:12:08.895 position:63%
It's what we're vitrifying.

00:12:08.962 --> 00:12:12.265 position:63%
That's the 56 million gallons
of high-level waste. Yeah.

00:12:12.332 --> 00:12:14.989 position:63%
-And it's just leaching into the ground
or what's happening now?

00:12:15.056 --> 00:12:18.671 position:63%
-No. So it, a little, it has.
-Depends what you ask too, right?

00:12:18.738 --> 00:12:20.301 position:63%
The tanks have leaked.

00:12:20.368 --> 00:12:23.176 position:63%
Sometimes the single-shell tanks
have leaked a bit.

00:12:23.251 --> 00:12:24.866 position:63%
Sometimes the double-shell tanks,

00:12:24.933 --> 00:12:26.913 position:63%
the first shell
has leaked into the second.

00:12:27.005 --> 00:12:30.783 position:63%
The big concern is that over time,
decades, hundreds of years, millennia,

00:12:30.850 --> 00:12:33.352 position:63%
if we don't do something
with that waste, it will keep leaking.

00:12:33.419 --> 00:12:35.755 position:63%
And it'll migrate
and it'll get in the water.

00:12:35.822 --> 00:12:39.625 position:63%
[Robert] So this is the world's first
full-scale nuclear reactor

00:12:39.692 --> 00:12:44.664 position:63%
built in about 11 months
from foundation to start the operation.

00:12:44.731 --> 00:12:46.399 position:63%
If you can believe that, 11 months.

00:12:46.466 --> 00:12:49.535 position:63%
It produced the plutonium
for the Trinity Test,

00:12:49.602 --> 00:12:53.772 position:63%
world's first test of an atomic weapon,
July 16th, Alamogordo, New Mexico.

00:12:53.839 --> 00:12:56.375 position:63%
Produced the plutonium
for the Nagasaki bomb.

00:12:56.442 --> 00:13:00.681 position:63%
And it operated from 1944 to 1968.

00:13:00.748 --> 00:13:04.550 position:63%
[Peter] Okay, so is this
the first plutonium reactor in the world?

00:13:04.617 --> 00:13:05.985 position:63%
-It's the first full-scale.

00:13:06.052 --> 00:13:07.730 position:63%
So there was a reactor at Oak Ridge

00:13:07.797 --> 00:13:10.901 position:63%
that produced really small samples
so they could do chemistry.

00:13:10.981 --> 00:13:12.263 position:63%
But this is the proof of concept

00:13:12.330 --> 00:13:14.402 position:63%
and all the other reactors
are modeled after this.

00:13:14.469 --> 00:13:16.495 position:63%
-Okay, what are these guys doing here now?

00:13:16.562 --> 00:13:19.332 position:63%
-[Robert] We are doing preservation work.
-[Kirk] Renovation.

00:13:19.399 --> 00:13:21.667 position:63%
-Renovation.
So they're repointing the grout.

00:13:21.734 --> 00:13:23.903 position:63%
And then they're repairing the roof.

00:13:23.970 --> 00:13:26.739 position:63%
So this is a
National Historic Landmark today.

00:13:26.806 --> 00:13:29.308 position:63%
Part of the Manhattan Project
National Historical Park.

00:13:29.375 --> 00:13:31.510 position:63%
You can imagine this was not meant to be

00:13:31.577 --> 00:13:34.269 position:63%
a National Historic Landmark
81 years later.

00:13:34.336 --> 00:13:35.392 position:63%
-Yeah.
-82 years later.

00:13:35.459 --> 00:13:38.484 position:63%
So there's this kind of
maintenance work that comes…

00:13:38.551 --> 00:13:42.488 position:63%
And one challenge, right, is this is
the first facility of its kind, so…

00:13:42.555 --> 00:13:45.592 position:63%
This is just work to preserve it
so people can visit?

00:13:45.659 --> 00:13:49.336 position:63%
-Yes. So the roof needs repair
and then the pointing on the grout.

00:13:49.403 --> 00:13:52.799 position:63%
-Okay, so this is Department of Energy?
-This is DOE, yes.

00:13:52.866 --> 00:13:56.002 position:63%
So you got your fans here
to intake and exhaust air.

00:13:56.069 --> 00:13:57.536 position:63%
You got your reactor here.

00:13:57.603 --> 00:14:01.140 position:63%
It's 45 feet tall by 45,
this giant reactor block.

00:14:01.207 --> 00:14:03.943 position:63%
They call it a pile
because it's a pile of graphite basically.

00:14:04.010 --> 00:14:05.378 position:63%
With a steel enclosure.

00:14:05.445 --> 00:14:09.922 position:63%
Then you got the back face, which is where
that irradiated fuel would shoot out,

00:14:09.989 --> 00:14:11.150 position:63%
sit in pools of water.

00:14:11.217 --> 00:14:15.187 position:63%
And then trains, like the train here,
right, would come and pick it up.

00:14:15.254 --> 00:14:19.291 position:63%
Those are lead-lined cask cars where
they would take this irradiated fuel.

00:14:19.358 --> 00:14:21.427 position:63%
It would be in water, lead-lined.

00:14:21.494 --> 00:14:24.462 position:63%
And then that train
would go up to the T Plant.

00:14:24.529 --> 00:14:27.433 position:63%
And it would back in
and they'd start these chemical baths

00:14:27.500 --> 00:14:31.771 position:63%
to dissolve those fuel elements
and isolate plutonium.

00:14:31.838 --> 00:14:34.407 position:63%
And then that would go to Los Alamos.

00:14:34.474 --> 00:14:39.178 position:63%
So this reactor creates the plutonium
and then what we can look at it like

00:14:39.245 --> 00:14:41.847 position:63%
if we go back to
archeological human civilization, right?

00:14:41.914 --> 00:14:45.551 position:63%
12,000, 14,000 years ago, right?

00:14:45.618 --> 00:14:49.021 position:63%
One half-life of plutonium is double that.

00:14:49.088 --> 00:14:51.315 position:63%
So when we created this plutonium here,

00:14:51.382 --> 00:14:54.124 position:63%
we created something
that we have to manage

00:14:54.191 --> 00:14:57.129 position:63%
for many times the lengths
that we have existed

00:14:57.196 --> 00:15:00.499 position:63%
as a coherent civilization
that we can measure.

00:15:00.566 --> 00:15:02.284 position:63%
How do you manage it right now?

00:15:02.351 --> 00:15:05.204 position:63%
-[Kirk] It's in weapons.
-Well, it's in weapons.

00:15:05.271 --> 00:15:09.308 position:63%
And our various countries
manage our nuclear weapons.

00:15:09.375 --> 00:15:13.746 position:63%
But, man, what about
2,000, 5,000, 10,000 years later?

00:15:13.813 --> 00:15:18.613 position:63%
Those are those deep time questions
that are wild to think about.

00:15:18.680 --> 00:15:23.656 position:63%
I'm sure there's an excess of plutonium
that's not in weapons. Where is it stored?

00:15:23.723 --> 00:15:26.893 position:63%
[Kirk] Oh, no.
When they develop a new weapon,

00:15:26.960 --> 00:15:30.697 position:63%
they actually take the plutonium
from the older weapons and put it in.

00:15:30.764 --> 00:15:33.934 position:63%
-So every bit of plutonium
is currently in a weapon?

00:15:34.001 --> 00:15:39.146 position:63%
Most of it. There is a little bit of
reserve, but most of it is in active use.

00:15:39.213 --> 00:15:44.110 position:63%
When they went through the nuclear
nonproliferation treaty with Russia,

00:15:44.177 --> 00:15:45.813 position:63%
that's when they stopped all production.

00:15:45.880 --> 00:15:50.216 position:63%
Because we have more than that can kill
the world a couple times, right?

00:15:50.283 --> 00:15:53.219 position:63%
So what they've done now
is they're taking their weapons

00:15:53.286 --> 00:15:56.991 position:63%
and if they develop a new weapon,
they just use that material.

00:15:57.058 --> 00:15:58.924 position:63%
-Where are we at with that?

00:15:59.007 --> 00:16:00.610 position:63%
We have about 3,000.

00:16:00.677 --> 00:16:05.056 position:63%
State Department
official figures about 3,175 nukes.

00:16:05.123 --> 00:16:07.833 position:63%
That includes
tactical and strategic nukes.

00:16:07.900 --> 00:16:11.203 position:63%
Then a variety of delivery systems.
It's called the nuclear triad.

00:16:11.270 --> 00:16:13.839 position:63%
You got your land, your sea and your air.

00:16:13.906 --> 00:16:16.440 position:63%
-What about Russia?
-Russia's got more.

00:16:17.443 --> 00:16:19.864 position:63%
They have dual-function reactors.

00:16:19.931 --> 00:16:23.149 position:63%
They go ahead and use the steam
to generate power,

00:16:23.223 --> 00:16:26.337 position:63%
but they're also using the spent fuel
to extract…

00:16:26.404 --> 00:16:27.407 position:63%
Russia's got about…

00:16:27.474 --> 00:16:30.554 position:63%
They say… I think our best guess
is close to double us,

00:16:30.621 --> 00:16:34.827 position:63%
but ours, we have a little more advanced
delivery systems, but it doesn't…

00:16:34.894 --> 00:16:36.996 position:63%
I mean, it doesn't really matter.

00:16:37.063 --> 00:16:38.597 position:63%
-It doesn't matter.
-It doesn't matter.

00:16:38.664 --> 00:16:39.998 position:63%
-The world's over.
-I mean, yeah.

00:16:40.065 --> 00:16:41.500 position:63%
And it's not just us, right?

00:16:41.567 --> 00:16:45.028 position:63%
There's also India,
Pakistan, Israel, China,

00:16:45.095 --> 00:16:47.673 position:63%
United Kingdom, North Korea, France.

00:16:47.740 --> 00:16:51.557 position:63%
-Soon to be Iran.
-Right? Question mark. Who's next?

00:16:52.211 --> 00:16:54.410 position:63%
Here's the back face of the reactor.

00:16:54.477 --> 00:16:57.316 position:63%
And then this area
that sticks out over here,

00:16:57.383 --> 00:16:59.485 position:63%
that's where this train would back in.

00:16:59.552 --> 00:17:02.688 position:63%
There were folks downwind
who were impacted by Hanford's release.

00:17:02.755 --> 00:17:05.291 position:63%
There were people that got sick,
thyroid cancers,

00:17:05.358 --> 00:17:07.860 position:63%
melanomas, leukemias, carcinomas.

00:17:07.927 --> 00:17:08.927 position:63%
That's a fact, right?

00:17:08.994 --> 00:17:13.566 position:63%
There's folks, they've been in litigation,
DOE, very well documented.

00:17:13.633 --> 00:17:16.335 position:63%
And that's from those airborne releases.
The early years…

00:17:16.402 --> 00:17:18.037 position:63%
-What were the airborne releases from?

00:17:18.104 --> 00:17:20.906 position:63%
So when these reactors are operating,
it's not so much the reactor,

00:17:20.973 --> 00:17:22.557 position:63%
it's the 200 Area.

00:17:22.624 --> 00:17:23.943 position:63%
It's the chemical processing.

00:17:24.010 --> 00:17:26.834 position:63%
When they're using acids

00:17:26.901 --> 00:17:30.282 position:63%
to dissolve highly irradiated fuel,

00:17:30.349 --> 00:17:31.851 position:63%
the smoke that's coming out,

00:17:31.918 --> 00:17:33.719 position:63%
the gases that are
coming out of the stack,

00:17:33.786 --> 00:17:37.890 position:63%
the stack gases, early on,
they didn't know to filter them.

00:17:37.957 --> 00:17:39.457 position:63%
They were in a rush to build a bomb.

00:17:39.524 --> 00:17:41.560 position:63%
They didn't let the fuel decay
long enough.

00:17:41.627 --> 00:17:44.997 position:63%
Because what's in those gases is things
like radioiodine,

00:17:45.064 --> 00:17:46.298 position:63%
radioxenon.

00:17:46.365 --> 00:17:48.260 position:63%
Those are the two worst ones
for human health.

00:17:48.327 --> 00:17:51.570 position:63%
There's also stuff that
we're worried about from atomic testing,

00:17:51.637 --> 00:17:53.338 position:63%
like cesium and strontium.

00:17:53.405 --> 00:17:56.242 position:63%
But radioiodine is the worst
because iodine,

00:17:56.309 --> 00:18:00.079 position:63%
your body, why we've had
iodized salt, is your thyroid.

00:18:00.146 --> 00:18:02.248 position:63%
-Your immune system needs iodine.
-Yeah.

00:18:02.315 --> 00:18:06.085 position:63%
But radioactive iodine, the isotope,
and regular iodine, chemically the same.

00:18:06.152 --> 00:18:07.586 position:63%
Your body doesn't distinguish.

00:18:07.653 --> 00:18:11.357 position:63%
So if you're getting radioiodine,
your body will stick it in your thyroid.

00:18:11.424 --> 00:18:14.393 position:63%
If you're getting
radioactive cesium and strontium,

00:18:14.460 --> 00:18:16.862 position:63%
your body thinks that's calcium
or treats it similarly.

00:18:16.929 --> 00:18:19.832 position:63%
It puts it in your bones.
It puts it in baby's teeth.

00:18:19.899 --> 00:18:21.600 position:63%
-Right.
-This is the big legacy.

00:18:21.667 --> 00:18:24.503 position:63%
So early on, when Hanford's emitting

00:18:24.570 --> 00:18:27.840 position:63%
hundreds of thousands of curies
of radioiodine here,

00:18:27.907 --> 00:18:29.434 position:63%
that's spreading out into the…

00:18:29.507 --> 00:18:30.517 position:63%
All over this area?

00:18:30.584 --> 00:18:33.145 position:63%
And they had high stacks,
higher than that.

00:18:33.212 --> 00:18:35.514 position:63%
And sometimes the winds
would disperse it really well,

00:18:35.581 --> 00:18:37.983 position:63%
and sometimes we'd have… This is a bowl.

00:18:38.050 --> 00:18:40.986 position:63%
You have a temperature inversions here
where the weather sits.

00:18:41.053 --> 00:18:44.235 position:63%
Well, they didn't stop
producing plutonium then.

00:18:44.302 --> 00:18:45.658 position:63%
They just kind of let it happen.

00:18:45.725 --> 00:18:48.294 position:63%
And the Green Run, this infamous incident,

00:18:48.361 --> 00:18:51.376 position:63%
where they purposely released
high levels of radioiodine

00:18:51.443 --> 00:18:53.932 position:63%
to track it but didn't
let the surrounding people know…

00:18:53.999 --> 00:18:55.968 position:63%
-Why'd they purposely release it?

00:18:56.035 --> 00:18:58.604 position:63%
-Because we needed to get data.
-Data.

00:18:58.671 --> 00:19:02.029 position:63%
We needed to get data
because the Soviets had just tested a bomb

00:19:02.096 --> 00:19:04.226 position:63%
in 1948, 1949,

00:19:04.777 --> 00:19:08.847 position:63%
and we were curious about
how many they could produce.

00:19:08.914 --> 00:19:12.351 position:63%
They say, okay, we could fly
a spy plane over the Soviet Union

00:19:12.418 --> 00:19:14.586 position:63%
with a radiological monitor on it.

00:19:14.653 --> 00:19:17.189 position:63%
But how would we know
the data we're looking at?

00:19:17.256 --> 00:19:18.089 position:63%
-Right.

00:19:18.156 --> 00:19:21.561 position:63%
-So they said, let's create the conditions
we think the Soviets are doing.

00:19:21.628 --> 00:19:23.364 position:63%
We rushed during the war.

00:19:23.431 --> 00:19:25.229 position:63%
We processed green fuel.

00:19:25.296 --> 00:19:28.467 position:63%
We had major radioactive releases
during World War II.

00:19:28.534 --> 00:19:31.270 position:63%
We were almost sure the Soviets
are going to be doing the same thing,

00:19:31.337 --> 00:19:33.105 position:63%
because they dropped the plutonium bomb.

00:19:33.172 --> 00:19:35.441 position:63%
They tested a plutonium bomb. So we know.

00:19:35.508 --> 00:19:38.243 position:63%
So we say, let's do
how we think they're doing it.

00:19:38.310 --> 00:19:41.379 position:63%
Let's run green fuel.
It'll be really messy.

00:19:41.446 --> 00:19:43.381 position:63%
And we'll measure the mess.

00:19:43.448 --> 00:19:45.784 position:63%
And they didn't tell people.
It's called the Green Run.

00:19:45.851 --> 00:19:48.654 position:63%
-It's a very infamous Hanford…
-You can find info on it.

00:19:48.721 --> 00:19:52.324 position:63%
But the thing is knowledge about it
didn't come out until the mid-80s.

00:19:52.391 --> 00:19:54.293 position:63%
It was secret for decades.

00:19:54.360 --> 00:19:58.030 position:63%
So we recreated what we thought
their dirty process was,

00:19:58.097 --> 00:20:00.699 position:63%
but we did it
during a temperature inversion.

00:20:00.766 --> 00:20:04.236 position:63%
When that stuff didn't dissipate,
it collected and came down here.

00:20:04.303 --> 00:20:07.473 position:63%
-So this whole area…
So Richland, where we started…

00:20:07.540 --> 00:20:12.144 position:63%
Richland had measurable radiation
70 to 80 times above background.

00:20:12.211 --> 00:20:15.148 position:63%
Which is concerning
if you don't know that.

00:20:15.215 --> 00:20:18.114 position:63%
Because, you know,
what if you run a dairy?

00:20:18.584 --> 00:20:20.852 position:63%
What if stuff's been depositing on grass,

00:20:20.919 --> 00:20:24.556 position:63%
the cows are eating grass,
and then they're secreting that into milk?

00:20:24.623 --> 00:20:27.359 position:63%
That's the big concern is,
right, with these releases,

00:20:27.426 --> 00:20:30.529 position:63%
how is it gonna move
through the environment?

00:20:30.596 --> 00:20:33.599 position:63%
Look, man, the Hanford
and the Atomic Energy Commission

00:20:33.666 --> 00:20:37.569 position:63%
pioneered the discipline of ecology
because we're so concerned about

00:20:37.636 --> 00:20:40.539 position:63%
where is this radiation going
that we're releasing.

00:20:40.606 --> 00:20:42.274 position:63%
But we can also measure it.

00:20:42.341 --> 00:20:45.411 position:63%
And really, a lot of what we know
about ecology and the environment

00:20:45.478 --> 00:20:49.248 position:63%
comes from nuclear testing
and this kind of operations

00:20:49.315 --> 00:20:52.751 position:63%
because we produced this thing
that we could measure.

00:20:52.818 --> 00:20:56.321 position:63%
[Peter] So when the people building this
were building it,

00:20:56.388 --> 00:20:57.956 position:63%
what did they think they were building?

00:20:58.023 --> 00:20:59.358 position:63%
-They had no idea.
-They had no idea.

00:20:59.425 --> 00:21:01.827 position:63%
At the end of the day, they're talking
to their buddies like,

00:21:01.894 --> 00:21:04.062 position:63%
what are we doing out here
in the middle of the desert?

00:21:04.129 --> 00:21:07.666 position:63%
-There was a lot of messaging
about not talking to your buddies.

00:21:07.733 --> 00:21:10.329 position:63%
-[Kirk] You could not talk.
-[Robert] You would lose your job.

00:21:10.396 --> 00:21:11.737 position:63%
[Robert] There were investigators.

00:21:11.804 --> 00:21:14.073 position:63%
If they're talking about it
and somebody overhears it

00:21:14.140 --> 00:21:15.774 position:63%
who does have some technical knowledge,

00:21:15.841 --> 00:21:18.277 position:63%
they might be able to put things together
and spread that.

00:21:18.344 --> 00:21:22.181 position:63%
Hanford was so concerned about security
that outside of a lot of these buildings

00:21:22.248 --> 00:21:25.984 position:63%
during World War II,
they had little cartoon heads of Hitler

00:21:26.051 --> 00:21:28.024 position:63%
that would be nailed onto the building

00:21:28.091 --> 00:21:31.296 position:63%
with a big ear sticking out
to tell people…

00:21:31.363 --> 00:21:32.196 position:63%
-Seriously?!
-Yeah.

00:21:32.263 --> 00:21:34.059 position:63%
Or throwing lightning bolts at you.

00:21:34.126 --> 00:21:36.328 position:63%
We have them in our collection.
They're called Hitler heads.

00:21:36.395 --> 00:21:40.065 position:63%
You know, it's him with his mustache
and his comb-over with his big ear

00:21:40.132 --> 00:21:42.768 position:63%
because there's real espionage concern.

00:21:42.835 --> 00:21:45.003 position:63%
Now, as you know
from your Los Alamos episode,

00:21:45.070 --> 00:21:47.573 position:63%
we should have been a little more
worried about the Soviets

00:21:47.640 --> 00:21:49.575 position:63%
than we were of the Germans,

00:21:49.642 --> 00:21:53.078 position:63%
but we were doing this, right,
to beat Germany to a bomb.

00:21:53.145 --> 00:21:55.414 position:63%
-Well, at the time,
the Soviets were allies.

00:21:55.481 --> 00:21:57.750 position:63%
-They were, although…
-We needed them to beat Germany.

00:21:57.817 --> 00:22:00.486 position:63%
Although Groves didn't trust them,
like, one bit.

00:22:00.553 --> 00:22:02.721 position:63%
And it turns out he was right not to.

00:22:02.788 --> 00:22:05.324 position:63%
-Yeah, but without the Soviets,
that war wouldn't have been won.

00:22:05.391 --> 00:22:06.492 position:63%
They did the heavy…

00:22:06.559 --> 00:22:08.279 position:63%
-Hey, no, yeah.
-They did the heavy lifting.

00:22:08.346 --> 00:22:09.828 position:63%
The lost 25 mil!

00:22:09.895 --> 00:22:12.464 position:63%
-One out of every six Soviets
died in World War II.

00:22:12.531 --> 00:22:15.601 position:63%
One out of every 360 Americans
died in World War II.

00:22:15.668 --> 00:22:18.238 position:63%
I mean, yeah, the Soviets took the brunt.

00:22:18.305 --> 00:22:21.140 position:63%
But I'm just talking in terms of
the Manhattan Project,

00:22:21.207 --> 00:22:23.639 position:63%
that espionage of Los Alamos but we were…

00:22:23.706 --> 00:22:26.945 position:63%
A lot of the security
and secrecy stuff was about

00:22:27.012 --> 00:22:30.115 position:63%
keeping people from talking
and stopping any kind of espionage,

00:22:30.182 --> 00:22:33.118 position:63%
which we feared
was coming from Germany.

00:22:35.020 --> 00:22:37.789 position:63%
[Peter] All right, guys, a quick break
from the Manhattan Project

00:22:37.856 --> 00:22:39.591 position:63%
to Midtown Manhattan.

00:22:39.658 --> 00:22:42.895 position:63%
Many of you are looking
for holiday gifts out there.

00:22:42.962 --> 00:22:46.999 position:63%
So if you got a family member,
a friend, someone that loves the channel,

00:22:47.066 --> 00:22:50.469 position:63%
what not a better gift
than Your Fellow Americans?

00:22:50.536 --> 00:22:54.469 position:63%
Every Barnes &amp; Noble across the country,
they're selling it.

00:22:54.969 --> 00:22:58.811 position:63%
And so what they're running
for the holidays, limited number,

00:22:58.878 --> 00:23:00.579 position:63%
are signed copies.

00:23:00.646 --> 00:23:03.549 position:63%
[woman] It is a popular book.
I have seen a lot of people buying it.

00:23:03.616 --> 00:23:05.217 position:63%
At one point, we did sell out.

00:23:05.284 --> 00:23:08.387 position:63%
-So you did come in at the right time.
-Oh, cool.

00:23:08.454 --> 00:23:09.888 position:63%
-Oh, that's good to hear.

00:23:09.955 --> 00:23:12.891 position:63%
These are all signed.
Depends when you come here.

00:23:12.958 --> 00:23:14.927 position:63%
It might be out. It might still be here.

00:23:14.994 --> 00:23:18.030 position:63%
And if you want a signed copy,
you're not in New York City,

00:23:18.097 --> 00:23:20.098 position:63%
most of the Barnes &amp; Nobles, I believe,

00:23:20.165 --> 00:23:23.302 position:63%
will be having a copy
of the signed version.

00:23:23.369 --> 00:23:25.704 position:63%
You're gonna have to call or stop in.

00:23:25.771 --> 00:23:28.026 position:63%
And the next best thing is a non-signed
copy.

00:23:28.093 --> 00:23:29.826 position:63%
It's still a great gift.

00:23:29.893 --> 00:23:33.745 position:63%
And if you can't get to Barnes &amp; Noble,
Amazon's the quickest way.

00:23:33.812 --> 00:23:37.683 position:63%
And your local indie bookstore
just might have it too.

00:23:37.750 --> 00:23:42.016 position:63%
Thanks so much for supporting the channel.
Now back to the video.

00:23:43.022 --> 00:23:45.290 position:63%
[Robert] This would have been a stop
on the old railroad,

00:23:45.357 --> 00:23:46.658 position:63%
the pre-Manhattan Project.

00:23:46.725 --> 00:23:50.362 position:63%
This is where the Hanford Irrigation
and Power Company pump house is,

00:23:50.429 --> 00:23:53.065 position:63%
right next to the jumbo reactors.

00:23:53.132 --> 00:23:56.655 position:63%
K East and K West.
They're about 3,000 megawatts.

00:23:56.722 --> 00:24:02.882 position:63%
It was actually a competition
between the reactor managers to…

00:24:02.949 --> 00:24:05.444 position:63%
Who could produce the most spent fuel.

00:24:05.511 --> 00:24:08.714 position:63%
And then way in the background,
you can see N.

00:24:08.781 --> 00:24:11.884 position:63%
N stands for new.
It's a dual-purpose reactor.

00:24:11.951 --> 00:24:17.055 position:63%
It's not like Chernobyl, but it's similar
to Chernobyl in that it is a dual purpose.

00:24:17.122 --> 00:24:21.586 position:63%
So it produces energy and plutonium,
but it's not the same design.

00:24:21.653 --> 00:24:25.197 position:63%
What was the equivalent of this
in the Soviet Union? Was it Chernobyl?

00:24:25.264 --> 00:24:26.097 position:63%
Mayak.

00:24:26.597 --> 00:24:27.725 position:63%
-Mayak?

00:24:27.792 --> 00:24:30.535 position:63%
The Mayak site near Ozersk.

00:24:30.602 --> 00:24:36.207 position:63%
We have our own environmental record,
but Mayak is just a whole other…

00:24:36.274 --> 00:24:37.409 position:63%
-Next level?
-Oh.

00:24:37.476 --> 00:24:40.445 position:63%
They discharged waste
into the Techa River.

00:24:40.512 --> 00:24:42.314 position:63%
People were living downstream of it.

00:24:42.381 --> 00:24:45.984 position:63%
I mean, they had
a whole waste pond that exploded.

00:24:46.051 --> 00:24:50.856 position:63%
They had people working in facilities
with almost no protective gear.

00:24:50.923 --> 00:24:53.639 position:63%
They weren't great with safety
or ecology, that's for sure.

00:24:53.706 --> 00:24:57.274 position:63%
But also, if you worked there you were
one of the most privileged workers

00:24:57.341 --> 00:24:58.526 position:63%
in the Soviet system.

00:24:58.593 --> 00:25:02.175 position:63%
People living there got good apartments
and Belgian chocolate.

00:25:02.242 --> 00:25:03.075 position:63%
Yeah.

00:25:03.142 --> 00:25:05.280 position:63%
Look, just like people
living in Richland got

00:25:05.347 --> 00:25:07.873 position:63%
brand new alphabet houses,
good pay, right?

00:25:07.940 --> 00:25:09.141 position:63%
Job security.

00:25:09.208 --> 00:25:11.877 position:63%
Historian Kate Brown
calls it a plutopia, right?

00:25:11.944 --> 00:25:17.149 position:63%
This kind of plutonium utopia economy,
plutonium utopia society, right?

00:25:17.216 --> 00:25:19.251 position:63%
Built around the production of plutonium.

00:25:19.318 --> 00:25:23.689 position:63%
Think about Richland,
When GE operates it, 1955, say.

00:25:23.756 --> 00:25:27.426 position:63%
Everybody that lives in Richland
works for the Atomic Energy Commission.

00:25:27.493 --> 00:25:30.062 position:63%
It's a town where
everyone has a job, right?

00:25:30.129 --> 00:25:31.863 position:63%
They're all working around Hanford.

00:25:31.930 --> 00:25:34.533 position:63%
They're all quiet
and they're all doing this thing.

00:25:34.600 --> 00:25:37.736 position:63%
There's no homelessness, no one unhoused,

00:25:37.803 --> 00:25:39.938 position:63%
very little property crime
because everyone has a job.

00:25:40.005 --> 00:25:42.007 position:63%
It's this utopian… I'll say it.

00:25:42.074 --> 00:25:44.810 position:63%
It's like a socialist utopia
in those days.

00:25:44.877 --> 00:25:47.012 position:63%
And some of the old-timers
wouldn't like to hear that,

00:25:47.079 --> 00:25:49.848 position:63%
but when you have a town where
the government's giving everyone a job,

00:25:49.915 --> 00:25:51.683 position:63%
what do you call that, right?

00:25:51.750 --> 00:25:54.720 position:63%
And it's all around the secrecy
and everybody's compliant

00:25:54.787 --> 00:25:57.489 position:63%
because they're all working out here
and they're all messaged about,

00:25:57.556 --> 00:26:00.325 position:63%
we gotta fight the Cold War,
we gotta win the Cold War.

00:26:00.392 --> 00:26:03.215 position:63%
And the same thing's happening
in the Soviet Union.

00:26:03.282 --> 00:26:05.163 position:63%
The exact same thing, actually.

00:26:05.230 --> 00:26:06.948 position:63%
[Peter] So my question is this,

00:26:07.015 --> 00:26:09.334 position:63%
and obviously
you weren't around during that time,

00:26:09.401 --> 00:26:11.870 position:63%
you have all this pride
in what you're doing, right?

00:26:11.937 --> 00:26:13.205 position:63%
Building up these facilities.

00:26:13.272 --> 00:26:16.902 position:63%
And once the cat's out of the bag
and people know what they're doing,

00:26:16.969 --> 00:26:18.243 position:63%
I mean, you're creating

00:26:18.310 --> 00:26:20.846 position:63%
the biggest monster
the world's ever seen, right?

00:26:20.913 --> 00:26:24.760 position:63%
And so how do you square that with,
I got this great town,

00:26:25.260 --> 00:26:27.693 position:63%
I'm protecting the country with,

00:26:27.760 --> 00:26:30.288 position:63%
we're unleashing
this destruction in the world.

00:26:30.355 --> 00:26:33.658 position:63%
And like, I'm sure there were
some moral issues with some people

00:26:33.725 --> 00:26:36.461 position:63%
being like, oh man,
I can't believe I'm part of that, right?

00:26:36.528 --> 00:26:38.764 position:63%
Well, you know, I think there was,

00:26:38.831 --> 00:26:40.732 position:63%
like my friend Jim Stoffels,

00:26:40.799 --> 00:26:45.269 position:63%
who worked out in the 300 Area,
and he founded World Citizens for Peace

00:26:45.336 --> 00:26:49.363 position:63%
in the 1980s, which was this group
that worked to promote peace

00:26:49.430 --> 00:26:51.943 position:63%
and nuclear disarmament,
and he worked at Hanford.

00:26:52.010 --> 00:26:54.713 position:63%
He brought awareness to peace campaigns.

00:26:54.780 --> 00:26:57.981 position:63%
But, you know, look, a lot of people
were told that what they did

00:26:58.048 --> 00:26:59.317 position:63%
won World War II,

00:26:59.384 --> 00:27:02.487 position:63%
what they did was help
the United States win the Cold War.

00:27:02.554 --> 00:27:04.089 position:63%
A true service to the country.

00:27:04.156 --> 00:27:06.425 position:63%
-Oh, absolutely.
They were told that by their government,

00:27:06.492 --> 00:27:08.928 position:63%
and that's why, you know,
the mascot of Richland High

00:27:08.995 --> 00:27:11.229 position:63%
is still our mushroom cloud, right?

00:27:11.296 --> 00:27:13.865 position:63%
-Okay, the high school
has a mushroom cloud mascot?

00:27:13.932 --> 00:27:14.866 position:63%
-Yep.
-Still?

00:27:14.933 --> 00:27:16.134 position:63%
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:27:16.201 --> 00:27:20.572 position:63%
Were you still here when we brought
Mr. Moriguchi, the Nagasaki survivor,

00:27:20.639 --> 00:27:22.074 position:63%
out to B Reactor?

00:27:22.141 --> 00:27:24.212 position:63%
Yeah, when they had
their little ceremonies

00:27:24.279 --> 00:27:25.310 position:63%
and told their stories.

00:27:25.377 --> 00:27:27.145 position:63%
-Yeah.
-Oh, that was amazing.

00:27:27.212 --> 00:27:29.397 position:63%
-What did he think
of the mushroom cloud mascot?

00:27:29.464 --> 00:27:30.516 position:63%
Well, he was interesting.

00:27:30.583 --> 00:27:32.918 position:63%
He was a Nagasaki survivor,
so the plutonium…

00:27:32.985 --> 00:27:35.387 position:63%
He was eight years old
when the bomb was dropped on him.

00:27:35.454 --> 00:27:37.923 position:63%
For him to go to the place
where they made that plutonium

00:27:37.990 --> 00:27:39.224 position:63%
was a very emotional moment.

00:27:39.291 --> 00:27:42.427 position:63%
But he also visited the high school
and tried to talk to people

00:27:42.494 --> 00:27:44.496 position:63%
about what the cloud meant to him.

00:27:44.563 --> 00:27:47.799 position:63%
And, you know,
it wasn't a great reception, honestly.

00:27:47.866 --> 00:27:49.439 position:63%
People weren't really…

00:27:49.506 --> 00:27:52.537 position:63%
There's still that…
this tension around that mascot,

00:27:52.604 --> 00:27:54.973 position:63%
where people from
outside this community don't get it.

00:27:55.040 --> 00:27:57.142 position:63%
Within the Tri-Cities,
not everybody agrees on it.

00:27:57.209 --> 00:28:00.145 position:63%
Okay,
but if I'm to get into that mentality,

00:28:00.212 --> 00:28:02.481 position:63%
this is what your town
was built up around.

00:28:02.548 --> 00:28:05.050 position:63%
-This is what your family did for work.
-Of course.

00:28:05.117 --> 00:28:06.585 position:63%
-This is our pride.
-Of course.

00:28:06.652 --> 00:28:08.653 position:63%
And you're gonna come in
and tell me to change it.

00:28:08.720 --> 00:28:09.554 position:63%
-Of course.

00:28:09.621 --> 00:28:11.223 position:63%
That would be the perspective, right?

00:28:11.290 --> 00:28:13.357 position:63%
It is. Think about monuments
in the South, right?

00:28:13.424 --> 00:28:18.296 position:63%
Think about if you're used to seeing
that Confederate soldier in that square,

00:28:18.363 --> 00:28:21.266 position:63%
and then somebody comes to tell you
it's wrong and they should change it.

00:28:21.333 --> 00:28:23.707 position:63%
And you might know,
there might be a part of you that says,

00:28:23.774 --> 00:28:26.771 position:63%
yeah, I don't agree with the history,
but it's part of what it is.

00:28:26.838 --> 00:28:30.876 position:63%
And you might defend it,
and that might be a bad look, right?

00:28:30.943 --> 00:28:33.259 position:63%
And to me,
it's that controversy around it,

00:28:33.326 --> 00:28:35.270 position:63%
the death, the destruction.

00:28:35.337 --> 00:28:36.474 position:63%
It brings it…

00:28:36.974 --> 00:28:39.584 position:63%
You know, it's that same kind of tension
that we have with like,

00:28:39.651 --> 00:28:42.354 position:63%
what does it say about
what we memorialize, right?

00:28:42.421 --> 00:28:44.052 position:63%
These things of power, symbols of power.

00:28:44.119 --> 00:28:47.125 position:63%
And then there's an interpretation,
let's say the Confederate flag,

00:28:47.192 --> 00:28:49.561 position:63%
you might look at that
just as Southern heritage.

00:28:49.628 --> 00:28:50.629 position:63%
I've met people that do.

00:28:50.709 --> 00:28:52.276 position:63%
Oh, of course. They do.

00:28:52.343 --> 00:28:53.733 position:63%
And that's their pride.

00:28:53.800 --> 00:28:59.404 position:63%
And then others might look at it
just as a flag of repression and racism.

00:28:59.471 --> 00:29:01.373 position:63%
-So it just…
-Or folks that went to battle

00:29:01.440 --> 00:29:03.441 position:63%
against the United States of America.

00:29:03.508 --> 00:29:06.344 position:63%
Or like, look, the Northerners, you know,

00:29:06.411 --> 00:29:09.047 position:63%
these city folks telling us
how to be and how to live.

00:29:09.114 --> 00:29:10.482 position:63%
-Yeah.
-We want our sovereignty.

00:29:10.549 --> 00:29:13.018 position:63%
I mean, that's part of this.
And this is a reflection of that.

00:29:13.085 --> 00:29:15.287 position:63%
But then again, that whole narrative
isn't from the war,

00:29:15.354 --> 00:29:20.292 position:63%
that's from the Reconstruction,
the battles over meaning and putting…

00:29:20.359 --> 00:29:21.891 position:63%
-That's a whole nother video.
-I know.

00:29:21.958 --> 00:29:24.438 position:63%
-That's a whole nother video,
but that's wrapping it into this.

00:29:24.505 --> 00:29:26.288 position:63%
It's the same controversy
around this mascot.

00:29:26.355 --> 00:29:27.188 position:63%
-Understood.

00:29:27.255 --> 00:29:28.718 position:63%
It hits all those same things.

00:29:28.785 --> 00:29:33.271 position:63%
All those same issues are right there
with what we call the R-Cloud.

00:29:33.338 --> 00:29:36.508 position:63%
[Robert] This area is a palimpsest.
There's indigenous history here.

00:29:36.575 --> 00:29:40.412 position:63%
And this rapids here, Coyote Rapids,
was the site of a longhouse

00:29:40.479 --> 00:29:42.747 position:63%
of the Wanapum prophet Smohalla.

00:29:42.814 --> 00:29:44.282 position:63%
We also have the agriculture, right?

00:29:44.349 --> 00:29:45.357 position:63%
You're looking at it.

00:29:45.424 --> 00:29:47.504 position:63%
-The Hanford irrigation.
-All across the river there?

00:29:47.571 --> 00:29:50.922 position:63%
Yeah, back there, modern agriculture,
historic agriculture, the pump house.

00:29:50.989 --> 00:29:52.691 position:63%
This pump house brought water up.

00:29:52.758 --> 00:29:54.659 position:63%
-Yeah, this was pre.
-1908.

00:29:54.726 --> 00:29:58.056 position:63%
-It brought water up
to this head canal here.

00:29:58.664 --> 00:30:00.198 position:63%
And then the nuclear.

00:30:00.265 --> 00:30:01.099 position:63%
-What was that?

00:30:01.179 --> 00:30:03.401 position:63%
That's the N Reactor.
The N Reactor you see right there.

00:30:03.468 --> 00:30:06.104 position:63%
The source is Eisenhower.
That's Atoms for Peace.

00:30:06.171 --> 00:30:09.509 position:63%
Kennedy, he talked about peaceful uses
of energy building on Eisenhower,

00:30:09.576 --> 00:30:14.246 position:63%
but he was here to start
the steam generation plant.

00:30:14.313 --> 00:30:16.815 position:63%
-The dual…
-The dual part to produce electricity…

00:30:16.882 --> 00:30:20.285 position:63%
…that America would be, you know,
we're still producing weapons,

00:30:20.352 --> 00:30:23.460 position:63%
but that we're moving
into this age of energy production.

00:30:23.527 --> 00:30:27.720 position:63%
And it's actually September 1963,
two months before the assassination.

00:30:27.793 --> 00:30:29.134 position:63%
-Oh, wow.
-He helicoptered in.

00:30:29.201 --> 00:30:32.452 position:63%
And this was really significant
because it was…

00:30:32.519 --> 00:30:33.931 position:63%
They allowed the public to come in.

00:30:33.998 --> 00:30:37.772 position:63%
So for many people,
kids, wives that lived in Richland,

00:30:37.839 --> 00:30:39.370 position:63%
it was mostly men
that worked out here,

00:30:39.437 --> 00:30:41.406 position:63%
but for their families
that lived in Richland,

00:30:41.473 --> 00:30:44.442 position:63%
this is the first time they were able to
even come out to the Hanford site.

00:30:44.509 --> 00:30:47.212 position:63%
And they sat there, thousands of them.

00:30:47.279 --> 00:30:49.080 position:63%
This helicopter came down, right?

00:30:49.147 --> 00:30:51.750 position:63%
And Kennedy came out.
The speech is on YouTube.

00:30:51.817 --> 00:30:53.986 position:63%
-It's recorded and there's tons of photos.
-Oh, cool.

00:30:54.053 --> 00:30:56.322 position:63%
And then he got in a helicopter
and zoomed off.

00:30:56.389 --> 00:31:00.174 position:63%
And then two months later in Dallas,
he's gone.

00:31:01.734 --> 00:31:03.757 position:63%
[Robert] So what you have
in this one landscape

00:31:03.824 --> 00:31:09.301 position:63%
where you can do a 360 is you have layers
of history one on top of the other

00:31:09.368 --> 00:31:13.438 position:63%
that you can choose to read
a specific layer if you want,

00:31:13.505 --> 00:31:15.840 position:63%
but they're all in this one place.

00:31:15.907 --> 00:31:17.609 position:63%
-What's the next layer gonna be?

00:31:17.676 --> 00:31:19.209 position:63%
That's a good question.

00:31:20.064 --> 00:31:22.356 position:63%
New nuclear, data center?

00:31:22.909 --> 00:31:23.752 position:63%
I don't know.

00:31:23.819 --> 00:31:25.029 position:63%
Is that in the talks?

00:31:25.096 --> 00:31:28.586 position:63%
There's been talks in West Richland
of data centers. Yeah.

00:31:28.653 --> 00:31:32.891 position:63%
I think people are pretty excited
for the next generation of reactors.

00:31:32.965 --> 00:31:36.307 position:63%
The data centers is
a totally different conversation

00:31:36.374 --> 00:31:37.697 position:63%
because that's not real…

00:31:37.764 --> 00:31:40.298 position:63%
The same, you know, the nuke reactors
might power those things,

00:31:40.365 --> 00:31:44.069 position:63%
but this, this next generation
of nuclear, you know, if this takes off,

00:31:44.136 --> 00:31:49.808 position:63%
this gives us real chances to move into
a new phase of energy generation.

00:31:49.875 --> 00:31:50.709 position:63%
-And nuclear now,

00:31:50.776 --> 00:31:53.311 position:63%
so we're talking about
all the fallout from nuclear

00:31:53.378 --> 00:31:55.513 position:63%
and the problems,
the things that can go wrong.

00:31:55.580 --> 00:32:01.086 position:63%
Nuclear done now, though there is risk,
it's still exponentially safer.

00:32:01.153 --> 00:32:04.889 position:63%
-Oh, dude. It's like comparing
a modern EV to a Model T.

00:32:04.956 --> 00:32:07.812 position:63%
-I mean, these reactors, these old guys…
-These are Model Ts?

00:32:07.879 --> 00:32:09.761 position:63%
This is the first generation.

00:32:09.828 --> 00:32:11.296 position:63%
We're so beyond this.

00:32:11.363 --> 00:32:12.823 position:63%
The waste that these…

00:32:12.890 --> 00:32:16.534 position:63%
Even the first commercial ones
from the 1950s and 60s.

00:32:16.601 --> 00:32:19.070 position:63%
I mean, we're still running
an old commercial reactor.

00:32:19.137 --> 00:32:21.606 position:63%
We have the one operating nuclear reactor
on the Hanford site.

00:32:21.673 --> 00:32:23.541 position:63%
-[Kirk] 1,200 megawatts.
-1,200 megawatt.

00:32:23.608 --> 00:32:26.010 position:63%
That is decades-old technology.

00:32:26.077 --> 00:32:29.280 position:63%
The technology we have now
is so much more efficient.

00:32:29.347 --> 00:32:31.015 position:63%
-So you're a big proponent of nuclear?

00:32:31.082 --> 00:32:34.018 position:63%
I'm, I'm one of those rare people
that are on the left

00:32:34.085 --> 00:32:36.521 position:63%
that's also very comfortable with nuclear.

00:32:36.588 --> 00:32:40.125 position:63%
The fact that we have these conversations
about what do we do with nuclear waste

00:32:40.192 --> 00:32:42.827 position:63%
in 10 years, 100 years, 1,000 years,

00:32:42.894 --> 00:32:44.395 position:63%
we have that conversation about that.

00:32:44.462 --> 00:32:48.066 position:63%
We don't have it about coal or oil.
We live with the effects of it.

00:32:48.133 --> 00:32:50.702 position:63%
But the fact that we have that comfort,
that care about nuclear,

00:32:50.769 --> 00:32:53.037 position:63%
I think gives me more hope
for that technology

00:32:53.104 --> 00:32:55.607 position:63%
because we are concerned about its waste.

00:32:55.674 --> 00:32:58.143 position:63%
We will do something proactive about it.

00:32:58.210 --> 00:32:59.744 position:63%
What's the alternative?

00:32:59.811 --> 00:33:02.950 position:63%
-The alternative is more carbon emissions?
-Right.

00:33:03.017 --> 00:33:07.066 position:63%
Decaying, you know,
wind plants or rivers without salmon.

00:33:07.133 --> 00:33:10.822 position:63%
I mean, the one thing that does make me,
give me hope on nuclear

00:33:10.889 --> 00:33:13.591 position:63%
is that a lot of the fuel chain
is domestic.

00:33:13.658 --> 00:33:17.028 position:63%
We don't have to have
these international concerns

00:33:17.095 --> 00:33:18.464 position:63%
or partnerships with countries

00:33:18.531 --> 00:33:20.853 position:63%
that we've had problematic
relationships with

00:33:20.920 --> 00:33:24.787 position:63%
and weapons sales
or this whole Strait of Hormuz situation.

00:33:25.570 --> 00:33:29.773 position:63%
We could get away from a lot of that
with a domestic fuel chain.

00:33:29.840 --> 00:33:31.742 position:63%
We'd have to
do that domestic fuel chain right,

00:33:31.809 --> 00:33:34.679 position:63%
but there's a way
to fashion a different world.

00:33:34.746 --> 00:33:36.581 position:63%
-And you'd consider yourself a green.

00:33:36.648 --> 00:33:38.583 position:63%
Oh, I'm very much an environmentalist.

00:33:38.650 --> 00:33:42.354 position:63%
I care a lot,
and I'm pretty left in my politics,

00:33:42.421 --> 00:33:47.158 position:63%
but I really believe the nuclear power
is a significant answer.

00:33:47.225 --> 00:33:48.689 position:63%
Nothing's ever 100%.

00:33:48.756 --> 00:33:54.032 position:63%
Every human system has error but to me
I'd rather put my chips in with that

00:33:54.099 --> 00:33:56.905 position:63%
than in this continued carbon emissions.

00:34:02.307 --> 00:34:04.776 position:63%
-[Peter] More reactors back here?
-[Robert] Yes.

00:34:04.843 --> 00:34:05.903 position:63%
And they just keep going.

00:34:05.970 --> 00:34:10.860 position:63%
I mean, we're talking
huge gaps in space, many miles…

00:34:10.927 --> 00:34:13.918 position:63%
-There's another one down there.
-Between all of these different reactors.

00:34:13.985 --> 00:34:17.589 position:63%
These are the different areas.
That's why it's the B Area, C Area, D.

00:34:17.656 --> 00:34:19.624 position:63%
Way out there. F and H.

00:34:19.691 --> 00:34:23.094 position:63%
And it wasn't here,
but didn't the Japanese send a, like…

00:34:23.161 --> 00:34:25.597 position:63%
-A Fu-Go bomb.
-Fu-Go bomb, right?

00:34:25.664 --> 00:34:26.965 position:63%
It's sort of like an air balloon

00:34:27.032 --> 00:34:29.834 position:63%
that got caught up
in some of these wires out here.

00:34:29.901 --> 00:34:33.438 position:63%
And you know what's crazy
in a little bit of historical irony,

00:34:33.505 --> 00:34:35.974 position:63%
those were launched
from Fukushima Prefecture.

00:34:36.041 --> 00:34:36.875 position:63%
Wow.

00:34:36.942 --> 00:34:39.744 position:63%
I've actually been to the place
where they launched them.

00:34:39.811 --> 00:34:43.948 position:63%
So the Japanese knew that was happening
here or they were trying to spy?

00:34:44.015 --> 00:34:45.149 position:63%
No, they didn't know at all.

00:34:45.229 --> 00:34:48.548 position:63%
They just wanted to
create a sense of fear.

00:34:48.615 --> 00:34:50.221 position:63%
They wanted to create panic.

00:34:50.288 --> 00:34:52.457 position:63%
Well, how did they know
to send the balloons over here?

00:34:52.524 --> 00:34:55.660 position:63%
-Because they went
wherever the jet stream took the balloons.

00:34:55.727 --> 00:34:57.996 position:63%
The Japanese had been doing
more work on the jet stream.

00:34:58.063 --> 00:35:01.332 position:63%
They knew of a jet stream before
American scientists really understood

00:35:01.399 --> 00:35:03.101 position:63%
the implications of the jet stream.

00:35:03.168 --> 00:35:05.103 position:63%
I mean, a bunch of those balloons
didn't make it,

00:35:05.170 --> 00:35:07.639 position:63%
but they knew of the jet stream,
and we didn't.

00:35:07.706 --> 00:35:10.625 position:63%
-They didn't know of this facility
and it randomly ended up here?

00:35:10.692 --> 00:35:14.612 position:63%
No. And they actually ended up
stopping production very briefly

00:35:14.679 --> 00:35:16.614 position:63%
when they knocked out the power lines.

00:35:16.681 --> 00:35:17.515 position:63%
-Oh, wow.

00:35:17.582 --> 00:35:19.284 position:63%
-A set of power lines for the B Reactor.

00:35:19.351 --> 00:35:21.686 position:63%
-That must put a scare here. What's this?
-That's the bank.

00:35:21.753 --> 00:35:23.221 position:63%
We'll come back to the bank.

00:35:23.288 --> 00:35:25.223 position:63%
Okay. So this is old White Bluffs.

00:35:25.290 --> 00:35:27.458 position:63%
-You can see the trees.
-Sidewalks.

00:35:27.525 --> 00:35:29.995 position:63%
-Why are all these electrical lines
going through here?

00:35:30.062 --> 00:35:32.263 position:63%
-Because of the dams.
-The dams. Right.

00:35:32.330 --> 00:35:34.632 position:63%
[Peter] You're a huge
hydroelectric state, huh?

00:35:34.699 --> 00:35:36.935 position:63%
-Oh, huge.
-Oh, my gosh, dude. Yes.

00:35:37.002 --> 00:35:39.266 position:63%
[Robert] We produce
more hydropower than any other state.

00:35:39.333 --> 00:35:41.004 position:63%
[Kirk] 8 or 10 dams just on the Columbia.

00:35:41.071 --> 00:35:44.375 position:63%
And a bunch on the Snake.
All in the state of Washington.

00:35:45.213 --> 00:35:47.044 position:63%
[Robert] This is actually
an ancient crossing.

00:35:47.111 --> 00:35:48.202 position:63%
This section of the river,

00:35:48.269 --> 00:35:51.349 position:63%
the 50 miles that runs through Hanford
and goes through the Tri-City,

00:35:51.416 --> 00:35:53.764 position:63%
it's the only free-flowing,

00:35:54.421 --> 00:35:58.723 position:63%
non-tidal section of the Columbia
River left in the United States.

00:35:58.790 --> 00:36:02.827 position:63%
Right? It's the only place where the river
returns to its natural flow rate.

00:36:02.894 --> 00:36:04.829 position:63%
It's the great salmon spawning grounds.

00:36:04.896 --> 00:36:09.000 position:63%
This crossing area was an important
crossing area since time immemorial

00:36:09.067 --> 00:36:12.337 position:63%
because if you're trying to
cross people and horses,

00:36:12.404 --> 00:36:13.871 position:63%
this is a great place to do it.

00:36:13.938 --> 00:36:17.709 position:63%
So the reason why this is
an undammed stretch is because of Hanford.

00:36:17.776 --> 00:36:22.547 position:63%
We preserved this section,
just like all this landscape is preserved.

00:36:22.614 --> 00:36:27.078 position:63%
This is some of the largest
shrub-steppe habitat left in North America

00:36:27.145 --> 00:36:28.169 position:63%
because of Hanford.

00:36:28.236 --> 00:36:30.314 position:63%
Because it was withdrawn
from public consumption.

00:36:30.388 --> 00:36:31.923 position:63%
-So Hanford's
good for the environment.

00:36:31.990 --> 00:36:35.360 position:63%
Well, it's this irony of it, like,
it's environmental preservation

00:36:35.427 --> 00:36:38.029 position:63%
with a massive environmental cleanup.
Right?

00:36:38.096 --> 00:36:40.031 position:63%
And it's a cool frickin' tension.

00:36:40.098 --> 00:36:42.571 position:63%
This area of the Columbia is home to like

00:36:42.638 --> 00:36:45.870 position:63%
more than 80 nesting pairs
of bald eagles, for example.

00:36:45.937 --> 00:36:49.073 position:63%
It helped recover the bald eagle
in the state of Washington.

00:36:49.140 --> 00:36:50.375 position:63%
-This water's clean right now?

00:36:50.442 --> 00:36:51.275 position:63%
-Oh, yeah.
-Oh, yeah.

00:36:51.342 --> 00:36:53.146 position:63%
It's been decades since production.

00:36:53.213 --> 00:36:55.780 position:63%
Decades.
And there's been pump and treat, right?

00:36:55.847 --> 00:36:56.700 position:63%
We've… This is…

00:36:56.767 --> 00:37:00.618 position:63%
The river was a really early area
of environmental cleanup.

00:37:00.685 --> 00:37:02.120 position:63%
-Kirk, what were you going to say?

00:37:02.194 --> 00:37:06.357 position:63%
Well, when the water came out
of the reactor it was a single pass…

00:37:06.424 --> 00:37:09.394 position:63%
They were single-pass reactors.
All these reactors were.

00:37:09.461 --> 00:37:12.663 position:63%
When it came out it went into basins
and it was multiple basins.

00:37:12.730 --> 00:37:15.099 position:63%
And they were checked
but it was very quick.

00:37:15.166 --> 00:37:17.902 position:63%
It wasn't directly out of the reactor

00:37:17.969 --> 00:37:20.377 position:63%
at 200 degrees into the river.

00:37:20.872 --> 00:37:23.240 position:63%
But if they had all nine reactors running,

00:37:23.307 --> 00:37:27.412 position:63%
they would see two or three degrees
difference down in Richland.

00:37:27.479 --> 00:37:29.414 position:63%
-When you're talking about this volume…

00:37:29.481 --> 00:37:32.550 position:63%
[Peter] Then up here
it had to be tens of degrees warmer.

00:37:32.617 --> 00:37:33.507 position:63%
-Oh, probably.

00:37:33.574 --> 00:37:36.031 position:63%
-Well, I don't know,
but I think the average river temperature

00:37:36.098 --> 00:37:37.422 position:63%
increased by like two degrees.

00:37:37.489 --> 00:37:38.322 position:63%
-Yeah, two degrees.

00:37:38.389 --> 00:37:43.361 position:63%
But that's enough to start to mess with
the river ecosystem and the ecology.

00:37:43.428 --> 00:37:46.831 position:63%
Activation products went in,
but also they would use chemicals

00:37:46.898 --> 00:37:50.668 position:63%
to prevent corrosion in the process tubes,
and that stuff would wash in.

00:37:50.735 --> 00:37:54.439 position:63%
So pump and treat,
what we've been working on since the 90s,

00:37:54.506 --> 00:37:57.675 position:63%
to pump out contaminated water
and then put in clean water,

00:37:57.742 --> 00:38:02.146 position:63%
was a really early cleanup effort
to protect this river for everyone.

00:38:04.849 --> 00:38:06.517 position:63%
[Peter] These trees were producing fruit?

00:38:06.584 --> 00:38:08.552 position:63%
[Kirk] Oh, yeah.
A lot of them, they just cut off.

00:38:08.619 --> 00:38:09.452 position:63%
-Why?

00:38:09.519 --> 00:38:13.191 position:63%
[Robert] So during the war, the first year
before production, summer of '44,

00:38:13.258 --> 00:38:18.229 position:63%
they actually brought low-risk prisoners
from McNeil Island on the west side

00:38:18.296 --> 00:38:19.297 position:63%
over here to pick fruit.

00:38:19.364 --> 00:38:22.033 position:63%
And they housed them in a camp
called Camp Columbia.

00:38:22.100 --> 00:38:24.969 position:63%
Once the reactors started,
they didn't want people out here.

00:38:25.036 --> 00:38:26.873 position:63%
And that's why they took down the houses,

00:38:26.940 --> 00:38:29.499 position:63%
'cause they just didn't
want them to be attractions.

00:38:29.566 --> 00:38:31.476 position:63%
Oh, they bulldozed all the houses?

00:38:31.543 --> 00:38:35.513 position:63%
-Except for the… You're going to
the only pre-Manhattan buildings

00:38:35.580 --> 00:38:37.313 position:63%
that are left on the site.

00:38:38.116 --> 00:38:40.048 position:63%
[Robert] This is what
it would have looked like.

00:38:40.115 --> 00:38:42.453 position:63%
-This is White Bluffs.
-The sidewalks here?

00:38:42.520 --> 00:38:45.156 position:63%
-Yep, these are the old sidewalks
of the old town of White Bluffs.

00:38:45.223 --> 00:38:49.794 position:63%
So there's some folks
celebrating July 4th, 1917.

00:38:49.861 --> 00:38:53.564 position:63%
-And the government just paid for
everyone to leave or forced them.

00:38:53.631 --> 00:38:55.800 position:63%
-They were evicted.
-Didn't have a choice.

00:38:55.867 --> 00:38:57.135 position:63%
-There's no choice.

00:38:57.202 --> 00:38:58.469 position:63%
-A lot of people took it.

00:38:58.536 --> 00:39:01.373 position:63%
Some folks didn't want to leave,
but a lot did.

00:39:01.440 --> 00:39:02.907 position:63%
Some people were barely making it.

00:39:02.974 --> 00:39:04.675 position:63%
You know, they were happy
to be bought out.

00:39:04.742 --> 00:39:07.678 position:63%
So you know, because this is
a hard place to farm.

00:39:07.745 --> 00:39:09.647 position:63%
This would have been
the commercial core, right?

00:39:09.714 --> 00:39:12.717 position:63%
General store,
ice cream parlor, drugstore.

00:39:12.784 --> 00:39:16.153 position:63%
In fact, this was the largest
of the three towns,

00:39:16.220 --> 00:39:17.851 position:63%
Hanford, White Bluffs, Richland.

00:39:17.918 --> 00:39:19.590 position:63%
This was the largest of the three.

00:39:19.657 --> 00:39:22.277 position:63%
And how much of a heads-up did they get

00:39:22.344 --> 00:39:24.695 position:63%
when the US government came in here?

00:39:24.762 --> 00:39:27.732 position:63%
Um, some as little as like 30 days
or a few weeks.

00:39:27.799 --> 00:39:32.069 position:63%
Some were allowed to stay until
right before the reactors went online.

00:39:32.156 --> 00:39:34.505 position:63%
It depended on
where your land was located.

00:39:34.572 --> 00:39:36.574 position:63%
If you had land that was gonna be

00:39:36.641 --> 00:39:40.178 position:63%
where they were gonna
build something right away, you're gone.

00:39:40.245 --> 00:39:42.527 position:63%
If you had land that was not near that

00:39:42.594 --> 00:39:44.615 position:63%
and DuPont hired you,

00:39:44.682 --> 00:39:47.251 position:63%
like the great example
is the Borden family.

00:39:47.318 --> 00:39:48.586 position:63%
They ran the ferry.

00:39:48.653 --> 00:39:52.723 position:63%
Archie Borden also ran the ice house
at the White Bluffs train station.

00:39:52.796 --> 00:39:55.851 position:63%
-So you see this row of trees over here?
-Yep.

00:39:55.918 --> 00:39:57.995 position:63%
-That's where the old train platform is.

00:39:58.062 --> 00:40:01.478 position:63%
And they had an ice house
to put ice in refrigerated rail cars

00:40:01.545 --> 00:40:02.900 position:63%
to transport all this fruit.

00:40:02.967 --> 00:40:05.603 position:63%
So Archie Borden,
he knows how to run this ice house.

00:40:05.670 --> 00:40:07.007 position:63%
His family farm

00:40:07.968 --> 00:40:10.402 position:63%
is back over where F Reactor is.

00:40:10.875 --> 00:40:14.045 position:63%
They let him stay until just a few weeks
before the reactor went online

00:40:14.112 --> 00:40:16.347 position:63%
because he was working for DuPont.

00:40:16.414 --> 00:40:19.550 position:63%
But some folks had as little as
like three to four weeks.

00:40:19.617 --> 00:40:20.952 position:63%
They got the letter in the mail.

00:40:21.019 --> 00:40:23.421 position:63%
Surveyor came,
told them what their land was worth.

00:40:23.488 --> 00:40:25.356 position:63%
You could fight it, some people did.

00:40:25.423 --> 00:40:27.725 position:63%
But a lot of people
just took the offer and left.

00:40:27.792 --> 00:40:30.194 position:63%
And then the town just went away.

00:40:30.261 --> 00:40:33.698 position:63%
-Well, the government reused
a lot of the facilities.

00:40:33.765 --> 00:40:36.483 position:63%
But over time, the necessity went away

00:40:36.550 --> 00:40:39.937 position:63%
and the towns just slowly disappeared.

00:40:40.004 --> 00:40:43.341 position:63%
You know, buildings fell down,
fires destroyed a few,

00:40:43.408 --> 00:40:46.578 position:63%
and then eventually the government
just tore down what was left,

00:40:46.645 --> 00:40:49.658 position:63%
except for a few
important charismatic structures

00:40:49.725 --> 00:40:52.870 position:63%
like our beautiful
First Bank of White Bluffs.

00:40:53.384 --> 00:40:56.087 position:63%
[Peter] This still looks new.
It doesn't look old.

00:40:56.154 --> 00:40:58.656 position:63%
-Well, this is
a historical reconstruction.

00:40:58.723 --> 00:41:00.992 position:63%
-Oh, it's not the real bank?
-No, it is the bank.

00:41:01.059 --> 00:41:03.194 position:63%
Well, it's like the ship of Theseus.

00:41:03.261 --> 00:41:04.996 position:63%
Do you know what the ship of Theseus is?

00:41:05.063 --> 00:41:07.796 position:63%
-Explain.
-Ship of Theseus is this parable

00:41:08.566 --> 00:41:10.737 position:63%
of this ship that over time,

00:41:11.250 --> 00:41:13.317 position:63%
every board of it was replaced.

00:41:13.838 --> 00:41:16.374 position:63%
But it was still the same ship.

00:41:16.441 --> 00:41:18.142 position:63%
-It's like our bodies after seven years.

00:41:18.209 --> 00:41:19.677 position:63%
But is it the same ship?

00:41:19.744 --> 00:41:23.948 position:63%
Even if every single board was replaced,
but they never did away with the ship.

00:41:24.015 --> 00:41:25.683 position:63%
[Kirk] And then the floor.

00:41:25.750 --> 00:41:27.685 position:63%
[Robert] Your taxpayer dollars at work.

00:41:27.752 --> 00:41:30.121 position:63%
-Was it under budget?
-It cost a million dollars.

00:41:30.188 --> 00:41:31.689 position:63%
A million dollars?

00:41:31.756 --> 00:41:34.025 position:63%
You got a cool old safe, though.

00:41:34.092 --> 00:41:35.493 position:63%
-It is. This is original.

00:41:35.560 --> 00:41:39.297 position:63%
This building, when I got here,
the roof had caved in.

00:41:39.364 --> 00:41:41.832 position:63%
This back wall was coming undone.

00:41:41.899 --> 00:41:43.245 position:63%
It was falling apart.

00:41:43.312 --> 00:41:45.472 position:63%
-Okay, that's what it was. Okay.

00:41:45.539 --> 00:41:46.630 position:63%
-Yeah, here's the back.

00:41:46.704 --> 00:41:49.307 position:63%
-So this is what it was.
This is how you found it.

00:41:49.374 --> 00:41:51.974 position:63%
-And then it's now this.
-It's now this.

00:41:53.311 --> 00:41:57.511 position:63%
-Oh, wow, you got a sundog. See that?
-Yeah. That's really cool.

00:42:02.520 --> 00:42:04.855 position:63%
[Robert] This is the old
Hanford High School.

00:42:04.922 --> 00:42:08.259 position:63%
-This is the town of Hanford?
-[Kirk] Yeah, this is what's left, yeah.

00:42:08.326 --> 00:42:09.460 position:63%
-Okay.

00:42:09.527 --> 00:42:11.628 position:63%
[Robert] Yeah, Hanford was
smaller than White Bluff,

00:42:11.695 --> 00:42:15.366 position:63%
so only about 300, maybe 400 at its peak.

00:42:15.433 --> 00:42:18.269 position:63%
[Robert] This town was founded
in the early 1900s

00:42:18.336 --> 00:42:21.739 position:63%
as part of the Hanford Irrigation
and Power Company.

00:42:21.806 --> 00:42:25.276 position:63%
This high school was built in 1916
and it's this right here.

00:42:25.343 --> 00:42:29.213 position:63%
So you can see
we are standing right in front of it.

00:42:29.280 --> 00:42:31.549 position:63%
This is the northeast corner of the camp.

00:42:31.616 --> 00:42:36.320 position:63%
Largest trailer camp in the United States,
over 6,000 automobile trailers.

00:42:36.387 --> 00:42:38.356 position:63%
And so here's a shot of the old Hanford.

00:42:38.429 --> 00:42:40.991 position:63%
And you can see
these are a lot of the barracks

00:42:41.058 --> 00:42:43.461 position:63%
that are going up here and powerhouses.

00:42:43.528 --> 00:42:45.696 position:63%
And then some of the older Hanford stuff.

00:42:45.763 --> 00:42:46.964 position:63%
There's the canal, right?

00:42:47.031 --> 00:42:48.699 position:63%
See, they built a road over it here,

00:42:48.766 --> 00:42:51.602 position:63%
but there's the canal
that went all the way over there.

00:42:51.669 --> 00:42:53.484 position:63%
[Peter] How many people did this house?

00:42:53.551 --> 00:42:56.574 position:63%
[Robert] About 45,000 people.

00:42:56.641 --> 00:42:59.643 position:63%
-And that was right there?
-Yes. So it had everything.

00:42:59.710 --> 00:43:03.314 position:63%
It had a bank, it had a laundromat,
it had a Sears and Roebuck's,

00:43:03.381 --> 00:43:05.783 position:63%
it had a movie theater, auditorium.

00:43:05.850 --> 00:43:08.019 position:63%
Some important things, though,
to know about this place.

00:43:08.086 --> 00:43:11.421 position:63%
So there's people that came here
from all over the US to work.

00:43:11.488 --> 00:43:14.925 position:63%
This was an Army site
so it was a segregated site.

00:43:14.992 --> 00:43:17.361 position:63%
Hanford did hire African American workers.

00:43:17.428 --> 00:43:21.465 position:63%
It had to pay them equally
because of federal contracting laws

00:43:21.532 --> 00:43:24.802 position:63%
passed during the New Deal
by President Roosevelt.

00:43:24.869 --> 00:43:26.604 position:63%
But it did have segregated facilities.

00:43:26.671 --> 00:43:30.074 position:63%
So for example it had segregated
Christmas entertainment,

00:43:30.141 --> 00:43:32.543 position:63%
segregated barracks,
segregated mess halls.

00:43:32.610 --> 00:43:34.199 position:63%
There were two exceptions.

00:43:34.266 --> 00:43:35.679 position:63%
They didn't segregate the bathrooms

00:43:35.746 --> 00:43:38.916 position:63%
because that was
a hard thing to do on the job site.

00:43:38.983 --> 00:43:42.154 position:63%
And they didn't segregate
the baseball leagues.

00:43:42.753 --> 00:43:46.223 position:63%
America's pastime,
especially in the 40s, baseball,

00:43:46.290 --> 00:43:49.060 position:63%
different crafts
had their own baseball teams.

00:43:49.127 --> 00:43:52.196 position:63%
And Black laborers had their own team
called the Hanford Eagles.

00:43:52.263 --> 00:43:55.499 position:63%
And the Eagles won
the Hanford baseball championship.

00:43:55.566 --> 00:43:57.401 position:63%
And they played against other White teams.

00:43:57.468 --> 00:44:01.138 position:63%
And in 1944, right, Black teams
didn't play against White teams.

00:44:01.205 --> 00:44:04.942 position:63%
People are so surprised to hear about
segregation in Washington State.

00:44:05.009 --> 00:44:07.411 position:63%
But Hanford brought
thousands of African Americans.

00:44:07.478 --> 00:44:09.501 position:63%
-They're coming out of the Delta.

00:44:09.568 --> 00:44:11.949 position:63%
They're coming out of the southern cities.

00:44:12.016 --> 00:44:14.518 position:63%
-Coming north for the opportunity.
-Absolutely.

00:44:14.585 --> 00:44:17.054 position:63%
And then many stay
and they settle in East Pasco,

00:44:17.121 --> 00:44:19.190 position:63%
and they bring family members with them.

00:44:19.257 --> 00:44:21.992 position:63%
If you're a Black
or a poor White in the South,

00:44:22.059 --> 00:44:26.163 position:63%
you'd be making three times
to five times as much money here

00:44:26.230 --> 00:44:27.765 position:63%
as you would staying in the South.

00:44:27.832 --> 00:44:30.668 position:63%
So this, I mean this drew so many people.

00:44:30.735 --> 00:44:34.105 position:63%
And you're getting out of
your car camper in the morning,

00:44:34.172 --> 00:44:35.806 position:63%
going off to build a reactor.

00:44:35.873 --> 00:44:37.875 position:63%
-Yeah, or something else, yeah.
-Or something else.

00:44:37.942 --> 00:44:40.911 position:63%
And you're coming home at night,
sort of curious, what am I doing out here?

00:44:40.978 --> 00:44:42.246 position:63%
-Yeah, but you're not going to…

00:44:42.313 --> 00:44:44.014 position:63%
-And the money's great.
-The money's great.

00:44:44.081 --> 00:44:46.217 position:63%
You know it has something
to do with the government.

00:44:46.284 --> 00:44:49.553 position:63%
Maybe there's a show at the auditorium.
You go bowling to distract yourself.

00:44:49.620 --> 00:44:52.189 position:63%
And you don't talk because DuPont hired…

00:44:52.256 --> 00:44:54.525 position:63%
In fact I interviewed a guy
for the Oral History Project

00:44:54.592 --> 00:44:57.728 position:63%
whose father had been
the principal of this very school.

00:44:57.795 --> 00:44:59.930 position:63%
His father had been the principal
of this very school.

00:44:59.997 --> 00:45:02.700 position:63%
Came in 1942 to take this job.

00:45:02.767 --> 00:45:04.502 position:63%
Done a year later, right?

00:45:04.569 --> 00:45:07.471 position:63%
He took this job and when DuPont came in,

00:45:07.538 --> 00:45:11.275 position:63%
they actually hired him
to be an investigator.

00:45:11.342 --> 00:45:14.445 position:63%
And he would go out
and he'd go to bars or public places,

00:45:14.512 --> 00:45:17.348 position:63%
and he would try to get people
to talk about their job.

00:45:17.415 --> 00:45:19.884 position:63%
And if they would,
he'd write, he'd get their name,

00:45:19.951 --> 00:45:21.751 position:63%
and [snaps] they'd be gone.

00:45:22.415 --> 00:45:24.021 position:63%
-The next day.
-Wow.

00:45:24.088 --> 00:45:26.390 position:63%
So DuPont really had a big footprint here?

00:45:26.457 --> 00:45:29.493 position:63%
-They ran it.
-So the government subcontracted that out.

00:45:29.560 --> 00:45:31.228 position:63%
The Army Corps of Engineers oversaw it.

00:45:31.295 --> 00:45:33.230 position:63%
Just like there's
a small overseeing force.

00:45:33.297 --> 00:45:36.967 position:63%
There were several hundred
Army Corps personnel overseeing DuPont

00:45:37.034 --> 00:45:40.391 position:63%
and its tens of thousands of workers.
Yeah.

00:45:40.458 --> 00:45:43.140 position:63%
So these were all DuPont employees?
-Yeah.

00:45:43.207 --> 00:45:45.075 position:63%
-I never even think of DuPont like that.

00:45:45.142 --> 00:45:47.745 position:63%
Just, well, they didn't
want to be associated with it.

00:45:47.812 --> 00:45:50.054 position:63%
Um, you know, in World War I,

00:45:50.581 --> 00:45:53.217 position:63%
DuPont had supplied munitions
for the US government,

00:45:53.284 --> 00:45:57.821 position:63%
and there was some controversy over
the quality of some of those munitions.

00:45:57.888 --> 00:46:00.624 position:63%
In the 1930s a committee
had been formed in the Senate

00:46:00.691 --> 00:46:05.111 position:63%
under Senator Nye of Montana
to investigate war profiteering.

00:46:05.178 --> 00:46:08.031 position:63%
And he dragged DuPont executives
in front of this committee

00:46:08.098 --> 00:46:09.834 position:63%
and accused them of being war profiteers.

00:46:09.901 --> 00:46:12.770 position:63%
He actually, the words he said
were "merchants of death."

00:46:12.837 --> 00:46:13.670 position:63%
Okay.

00:46:13.737 --> 00:46:15.712 position:63%
So if you're DuPont,
do you think you want to take

00:46:15.779 --> 00:46:18.409 position:63%
the top-secret atomic bomb project? No.

00:46:18.476 --> 00:46:21.111 position:63%
But DuPont was also, what does DuPont do?

00:46:21.178 --> 00:46:22.546 position:63%
They're a chemicals company.

00:46:22.613 --> 00:46:24.648 position:63%
And at this time they own Remington.

00:46:24.715 --> 00:46:26.951 position:63%
So they're an engineering
and chemicals company.

00:46:27.018 --> 00:46:28.419 position:63%
Well, that's what we're doing here.

00:46:28.486 --> 00:46:32.122 position:63%
Yeah, this, there's nuclear reactors
but those reactors are producing a metal

00:46:32.189 --> 00:46:33.791 position:63%
that we're going to chemically separate.

00:46:33.858 --> 00:46:35.893 position:63%
This is chemistry
and engineering at Hanford.

00:46:35.960 --> 00:46:39.663 position:63%
That's what's different fundamentally
about this place in Los Alamos.

00:46:39.730 --> 00:46:41.865 position:63%
This is the realm
of the engineer and the chemist.

00:46:41.932 --> 00:46:44.999 position:63%
Los Alamos is the realm of the physicist.
Okay?

00:46:45.503 --> 00:46:46.637 position:63%
To put it really simply.

00:46:46.704 --> 00:46:49.974 position:63%
So DuPont says,
Groves goes to him basically,

00:46:50.041 --> 00:46:52.276 position:63%
he doesn't beg but he's like,
"We need you to do this."

00:46:52.343 --> 00:46:56.297 position:63%
And DuPont says, "All right, well, we
have to sign a memorandum of understanding

00:46:56.364 --> 00:46:58.789 position:63%
that you have to admit, government,

00:46:58.856 --> 00:47:02.586 position:63%
you have to admit that you don't know if
it'll work, we won't be held accountable,

00:47:02.653 --> 00:47:04.455 position:63%
and that we're not gonna profit from it."

00:47:04.522 --> 00:47:07.229 position:63%
DuPont symbolically only took…

00:47:07.691 --> 00:47:09.994 position:63%
Because the government
can't do a free contract,

00:47:10.061 --> 00:47:13.030 position:63%
they set DuPont's payment at $1.

00:47:13.097 --> 00:47:14.798 position:63%
-But would they get paid for this?

00:47:14.884 --> 00:47:18.284 position:63%
So the funny story is
so they set the profit at $1.

00:47:18.804 --> 00:47:22.506 position:63%
And because of taxes,
they got paid 67 cents.

00:47:22.573 --> 00:47:27.311 position:63%
So the Pasco Chamber of Commerce
in 1945 or 1946

00:47:27.378 --> 00:47:31.548 position:63%
took up a collection of 33 cents
to pay to DuPont

00:47:31.615 --> 00:47:34.338 position:63%
to symbolically get them to $1.

00:47:34.405 --> 00:47:36.919 position:63%
But what they were
really concerned about is

00:47:36.986 --> 00:47:39.690 position:63%
they didn't want to be seen as
being connected with the atomic bomb.

00:47:39.757 --> 00:47:41.692 position:63%
They didn't want
that "merchants of death"

00:47:41.759 --> 00:47:45.229 position:63%
because they're a company in markets
all over the world, right?

00:47:45.296 --> 00:47:46.196 position:63%
They don't want to be…

00:47:46.263 --> 00:47:48.283 position:63%
And so that's why
you've never heard of it.

00:47:48.350 --> 00:47:49.849 position:63%
Whereas General Electric,

00:47:49.917 --> 00:47:53.384 position:63%
when the Atomic Energy Commission
is created in 1947,

00:47:53.897 --> 00:47:55.917 position:63%
General Electric thinks
they might actually want to

00:47:55.984 --> 00:47:57.440 position:63%
get into this nuclear thing someday.

00:47:57.507 --> 00:48:00.444 position:63%
That there's money to be made here
but also nuclear energy.

00:48:00.511 --> 00:48:05.082 position:63%
Scientists… As soon as we had speculated
on the power of the atom,

00:48:05.149 --> 00:48:07.651 position:63%
of splitting the atom,
scientists were of two minds.

00:48:07.718 --> 00:48:10.888 position:63%
Some said, "This amazing amount of energy
could be harnessed into a weapon."

00:48:10.955 --> 00:48:13.223 position:63%
And many others said,
"Hey, this amazing amount of energy

00:48:13.290 --> 00:48:14.892 position:63%
could be used to make electricity."

00:48:14.959 --> 00:48:19.196 position:63%
And so part of the reason General Electric
comes in is they want that experience.

00:48:19.263 --> 00:48:22.501 position:63%
They're willing to embrace
what Hanford does

00:48:22.967 --> 00:48:25.969 position:63%
in order to get that experience
in the nuclear industry.

00:48:26.036 --> 00:48:29.373 position:63%
-What about how much
did DuPont make from this operation?

00:48:29.440 --> 00:48:31.275 position:63%
-Or that's unknown?
-They made nothing.

00:48:31.342 --> 00:48:32.784 position:63%
What do you mean they made nothing?

00:48:32.851 --> 00:48:35.579 position:63%
They did but it was cost plus $1.

00:48:35.646 --> 00:48:39.142 position:63%
They did it because of the patriotic…

00:48:39.616 --> 00:48:42.753 position:63%
I mean they didn't spend money to do it,
but they did it for cost plus $1.

00:48:42.820 --> 00:48:44.121 position:63%
-All salaries were paid?

00:48:44.188 --> 00:48:47.958 position:63%
Yeah, they wanted
to rehabilitate themselves as, you know,

00:48:48.025 --> 00:48:52.715 position:63%
and be not seen as war profiteers
but as a patriotic company.

00:48:57.835 --> 00:49:00.571 position:63%
[Robert] Peter, this road
that you're looking at,

00:49:00.638 --> 00:49:04.141 position:63%
this is the second four-lane highway
built in the state of Washington.

00:49:04.208 --> 00:49:07.344 position:63%
And that tells you
how many people were out here.

00:49:07.411 --> 00:49:11.014 position:63%
You had to build a four-lane highway
to get people through.

00:49:11.081 --> 00:49:15.681 position:63%
-The Hanford 500? Are you going to show us
what we can do in this van?

00:49:16.353 --> 00:49:18.598 position:63%
Government plates, maybe not a good idea.

00:49:18.691 --> 00:49:20.861 position:63%
-We're monitored.
-See right there?

00:49:20.928 --> 00:49:22.826 position:63%
[Robert] Vehicle data collection active.

00:49:22.893 --> 00:49:24.795 position:63%
-Vehicle data collection active.

00:49:24.862 --> 00:49:26.730 position:63%
That's going back to
the motherboard or what?

00:49:26.797 --> 00:49:29.032 position:63%
-It's got a GPS. This thing is monitored.

00:49:29.099 --> 00:49:33.303 position:63%
[Peter] So once those reactors
were built out, most of the workers left.

00:49:33.370 --> 00:49:34.705 position:63%
-They had to.
-Yeah.

00:49:34.772 --> 00:49:36.607 position:63%
[Robert] And then
it's an operations force.

00:49:36.674 --> 00:49:38.575 position:63%
-And then how big
is that operations force?

00:49:38.642 --> 00:49:41.512 position:63%
-They're in Richland,
and it's like 18,000 people,

00:49:41.579 --> 00:49:44.715 position:63%
but that includes women and children
'cause now that's your family.

00:49:44.788 --> 00:49:46.283 position:63%
But these structural workers,

00:49:46.350 --> 00:49:48.051 position:63%
they're mostly construction workers,
right?

00:49:48.124 --> 00:49:50.563 position:63%
-Okay.
-They're mostly the type of folks you find

00:49:50.630 --> 00:49:52.222 position:63%
in a company town, right?

00:49:52.289 --> 00:49:53.559 position:63%
Or like a mining site.

00:49:53.626 --> 00:49:55.826 position:63%
And there are a few families
in the trailers,

00:49:55.893 --> 00:49:58.729 position:63%
but it's mostly a transient workforce.

00:49:58.796 --> 00:50:02.566 position:63%
These operators that DuPont gets and later
General Electric to run this place,

00:50:02.633 --> 00:50:04.315 position:63%
there's a lot of young college grads.

00:50:04.382 --> 00:50:10.434 position:63%
In fact in 1950 Richland had the highest
birth rate per capita in the United States

00:50:10.501 --> 00:50:12.376 position:63%
because this place is full of…

00:50:12.450 --> 00:50:14.454 position:63%
-There's not a ton to do besides work…
-And f*ck.

00:50:14.521 --> 00:50:19.349 position:63%
And meet somebody or bring your family
here, the highest birth rate per capita.

00:50:19.416 --> 00:50:22.586 position:63%
-So there we are, out of the gate.
-We are out of the gate.

00:50:22.653 --> 00:50:25.022 position:63%
Way down over here on the right,
you can't quite see it,

00:50:25.089 --> 00:50:28.058 position:63%
but if we went down that way,
it would be the LIGO,

00:50:28.125 --> 00:50:31.428 position:63%
the Laser Interferometer
Gravitational Observatory.

00:50:31.495 --> 00:50:34.298 position:63%
It's our black hole detector,
gravitational wave detector.

00:50:34.371 --> 00:50:35.211 position:63%
Oh, no way.

00:50:35.278 --> 00:50:38.784 position:63%
Yeah. And it confirmed
the existence of black holes

00:50:38.851 --> 00:50:40.404 position:63%
the year I moved here, 2016.

00:50:40.471 --> 00:50:43.006 position:63%
And they've confirmed many more since.

00:50:43.073 --> 00:50:45.609 position:63%
There was plutonium separations
work done here.

00:50:45.676 --> 00:50:47.077 position:63%
[Kirk] My office was right there.

00:50:47.144 --> 00:50:50.848 position:63%
[Robert] The one on the left
had a cell that leaked into the ground

00:50:50.915 --> 00:50:55.064 position:63%
and it has hundreds of thousands of curies
of radiation below that building.

00:50:55.131 --> 00:50:55.964 position:63%
Whoa!

00:50:56.031 --> 00:50:58.355 position:63%
So much that they
can't get people down in there.

00:50:58.422 --> 00:51:00.524 position:63%
And so they're trying
to find the approach.

00:51:00.591 --> 00:51:02.893 position:63%
They can't even get robots down there.

00:51:02.960 --> 00:51:05.162 position:63%
So they have to figure out
how to take the building down

00:51:05.229 --> 00:51:08.098 position:63%
and then approach getting the soil
because it's right next to the river.

00:51:08.165 --> 00:51:09.666 position:63%
We're almost back in Richland.

00:51:09.733 --> 00:51:11.368 position:63%
-Yeah, that's PNNL right up here.

00:51:11.435 --> 00:51:13.036 position:63%
And the river is just right over there.

00:51:13.103 --> 00:51:15.205 position:63%
That bluff over there
is the other side of the river.

00:51:15.272 --> 00:51:17.441 position:63%
So that's a disaster over there.

00:51:17.508 --> 00:51:20.110 position:63%
-That is… That's a big concern.
-It is a big concern.

00:51:20.177 --> 00:51:22.446 position:63%
-Do you guys worry about this
living in town here?

00:51:22.513 --> 00:51:23.881 position:63%
I mean we're almost back in town.

00:51:23.948 --> 00:51:26.216 position:63%
-324 is concerning to me personally.

00:51:26.283 --> 00:51:28.986 position:63%
We don't get a lot of rain.
Things don't move but it is close.

00:51:29.053 --> 00:51:31.822 position:63%
The 200 Area and the tanks,
that's not that close.

00:51:31.889 --> 00:51:32.890 position:63%
That's a problem.

00:51:32.957 --> 00:51:35.525 position:63%
If you left it for hundreds of years,
thousands of years,

00:51:35.592 --> 00:51:37.227 position:63%
it would be a major problem.

00:51:37.294 --> 00:51:39.296 position:63%
324 is… It's close.

00:51:39.363 --> 00:51:41.231 position:63%
And I also worry about
how we're going to do it

00:51:41.298 --> 00:51:42.900 position:63%
because it's going to be dangerous work.

00:51:42.967 --> 00:51:44.968 position:63%
-And you have vineyards
right across the river.

00:51:45.035 --> 00:51:46.136 position:63%
-It's like right there.

00:51:46.203 --> 00:51:47.237 position:63%
-Oh, yeah, apples.

00:51:47.304 --> 00:51:49.640 position:63%
-Do the grapes grow better?
Do they grow quicker?

00:51:49.707 --> 00:51:51.842 position:63%
-They glow. They're great.
-They glow?

00:51:51.909 --> 00:51:54.144 position:63%
-Yeah.
-It's an ongoing issue.

00:51:54.211 --> 00:51:55.879 position:63%
-Yeah. That is…
-That was the tough one.

00:51:55.946 --> 00:51:59.416 position:63%
The big concern now is,
do they build a shield over it

00:51:59.483 --> 00:52:02.152 position:63%
and take down the building
or do they use the building as a shield

00:52:02.219 --> 00:52:03.654 position:63%
and dig up the soil beneath it?

00:52:03.721 --> 00:52:05.408 position:63%
-That's what they were…

00:52:05.475 --> 00:52:08.791 position:63%
Are people concerned in town?
Are they worried about this stuff or no?

00:52:08.858 --> 00:52:10.694 position:63%
-A lot more from outside of town.

00:52:10.761 --> 00:52:13.997 position:63%
Like folks like groups that I know
and work with like Hanford Challenge

00:52:14.064 --> 00:52:16.667 position:63%
are really trying to get people concerned.

00:52:16.734 --> 00:52:21.451 position:63%
But people in town
kind of don't really know about 324.

00:52:21.939 --> 00:52:24.708 position:63%
-Well, I think a lot of it…
I'd like to see data.

00:52:24.775 --> 00:52:29.502 position:63%
There were cesium cells
in the hot cells in that 324 building

00:52:30.047 --> 00:52:32.649 position:63%
that we actually sold over to Europe.

00:52:32.716 --> 00:52:34.685 position:63%
I can't even remember who it was to.

00:52:34.752 --> 00:52:37.539 position:63%
But they were so hot they were
never able to move them or anything,

00:52:37.606 --> 00:52:38.890 position:63%
do anything with them.

00:52:38.957 --> 00:52:42.893 position:63%
And I had crews of Nathan's that went in

00:52:42.960 --> 00:52:46.897 position:63%
and actually deconned those because
every time they do any work in there

00:52:46.964 --> 00:52:49.466 position:63%
it would contaminate
what they called their gallery

00:52:49.533 --> 00:52:52.603 position:63%
where they had their manipulators
where they were,

00:52:52.670 --> 00:52:54.871 position:63%
because you don't handle any of that.

00:52:54.938 --> 00:52:58.222 position:63%
You're looking through two feet of glass,

00:52:58.742 --> 00:53:02.676 position:63%
leaded glass and you're manipulating
with robot arms, right?

00:53:03.514 --> 00:53:05.015 position:63%
And they do all kinds of work.

00:53:05.082 --> 00:53:08.852 position:63%
You never brought anything out,
everything always stayed in.

00:53:08.919 --> 00:53:11.421 position:63%
[Peter] Okay, so this is
your national laboratory here?

00:53:11.488 --> 00:53:13.557 position:63%
-Yes. It's ran by Battelle.

00:53:13.624 --> 00:53:16.493 position:63%
-So a ton of jobs coming into town?
-Oh, a ton.

00:53:16.560 --> 00:53:19.763 position:63%
-And we're in this far-out desert outpost
in Washington.

00:53:19.830 --> 00:53:23.634 position:63%
-Yeah. We barely have an interstate.
We just got connected.

00:53:23.701 --> 00:53:26.203 position:63%
[Peter] It's just wild that the science
never stopped here.

00:53:26.270 --> 00:53:27.904 position:63%
It's like Los Alamos in that sense.

00:53:27.971 --> 00:53:30.007 position:63%
[Kirk] It's a lot like that. Yes.

00:53:30.074 --> 00:53:33.810 position:63%
-[Peter] A lot of new building here.
-[Kirk] That's all new building. Yeah.

00:53:33.877 --> 00:53:37.614 position:63%
[Peter] Wow.
So Richland's doing well economically?

00:53:37.681 --> 00:53:39.384 position:63%
-I tell you what, for the last 20 years,

00:53:39.451 --> 00:53:42.416 position:63%
it hasn't seen anything close
to a downturn.

00:53:45.889 --> 00:53:47.691 position:63%
[Robert] This is the sail,

00:53:47.758 --> 00:53:52.129 position:63%
the conning tower
of the USS Triton submarine.

00:53:52.196 --> 00:53:54.183 position:63%
This is the first
nuclear-powered submarine

00:53:54.250 --> 00:53:58.059 position:63%
to completely circumnavigate
the globe underwater.

00:53:58.502 --> 00:54:00.504 position:63%
-Had two reactors.
-Had two reactors.

00:54:00.571 --> 00:54:02.304 position:63%
Here you can see the path.

00:54:03.340 --> 00:54:04.808 position:63%
There's the big cranes.

00:54:04.875 --> 00:54:07.273 position:63%
There's some giant transport trucks.

00:54:07.911 --> 00:54:10.861 position:63%
Hanford is the home of our nation's

00:54:10.928 --> 00:54:15.519 position:63%
spent carrier nuclear ship
and nuclear sub cores.

00:54:15.586 --> 00:54:18.855 position:63%
When these vessels are decommissioned,
whether it's a sub or a ship,

00:54:18.922 --> 00:54:20.790 position:63%
they basically cut the core out.

00:54:20.857 --> 00:54:22.059 position:63%
They seal it up.

00:54:22.126 --> 00:54:24.061 position:63%
They bring it over to the West Coast.

00:54:24.128 --> 00:54:28.398 position:63%
They put it on a barge all the way up
the Columbia and it unloads right here.

00:54:28.465 --> 00:54:32.302 position:63%
They close down roads for a day,
usually on a weekend.

00:54:32.369 --> 00:54:34.905 position:63%
And then they bring it up to the 200 Area

00:54:34.972 --> 00:54:37.541 position:63%
where the processing is,
where the waste is stored.

00:54:37.608 --> 00:54:39.209 position:63%
And they put it in a pit.

00:54:39.276 --> 00:54:40.280 position:63%
They're not covered.

00:54:40.347 --> 00:54:44.715 position:63%
They're open so that satellites,
due to some certain treaties we have,

00:54:44.782 --> 00:54:49.753 position:63%
our friends and enemies can count how many
nuclear ships we've decommissioned,

00:54:49.820 --> 00:54:51.455 position:63%
to keep track of our…

00:54:51.522 --> 00:54:53.690 position:63%
And we have the same arrangements
with other countries.

00:54:53.757 --> 00:54:55.292 position:63%
Okay, how many have we decommissioned?

00:54:55.359 --> 00:54:57.728 position:63%
How many a year, for example,
come through here?

00:54:57.795 --> 00:55:00.363 position:63%
I'm not sure per year
but there are a couple,

00:55:00.430 --> 00:55:03.101 position:63%
there's almost about 200 of them up there.

00:55:03.168 --> 00:55:04.201 position:63%
[Kirk] Almost 200 of 'em.

00:55:04.268 --> 00:55:06.369 position:63%
We've been taking these things in
for decades.

00:55:06.436 --> 00:55:09.840 position:63%
And is it just because Hanford site,
it's already radioactive,

00:55:09.907 --> 00:55:11.408 position:63%
let's just put some more waste there?

00:55:11.475 --> 00:55:13.677 position:63%
I'm not sure if
it's an exact decision to make it.

00:55:13.744 --> 00:55:15.078 position:63%
-I don't know how it was made.

00:55:15.145 --> 00:55:16.713 position:63%
But it makes a lot of sense.

00:55:16.780 --> 00:55:19.382 position:63%
We got a lot of land,
we're already storing waste.

00:55:19.449 --> 00:55:23.253 position:63%
Somehow the decision was made
and so we are one of the rare sites

00:55:23.320 --> 00:55:26.356 position:63%
that actually takes in nuclear waste.

00:55:26.423 --> 00:55:28.859 position:63%
I mean you could consider them
nuclear waste, right?

00:55:28.926 --> 00:55:31.862 position:63%
It's disposal
and so we still take that in.

00:55:31.929 --> 00:55:34.965 position:63%
[Peter] The laboratory
is the biggest employer here.

00:55:35.032 --> 00:55:36.800 position:63%
It's funding these high salaries, right?

00:55:36.867 --> 00:55:38.401 position:63%
For homes like this?

00:55:38.468 --> 00:55:39.469 position:63%
-Some of it, yeah.

00:55:39.536 --> 00:55:43.039 position:63%
Well, our congressional representative
used to live over here.

00:55:43.106 --> 00:55:45.175 position:63%
-Doc Hastings.
-Old Doc Hastings.

00:55:45.242 --> 00:55:48.678 position:63%
You know where he made his money.
That's how they all make their money.

00:55:48.745 --> 00:55:52.082 position:63%
Some of this is scientist money,
some of it's ag money,

00:55:52.149 --> 00:55:53.550 position:63%
some of it's tech money.

00:55:53.617 --> 00:55:57.654 position:63%
You know, we're a pretty
diversified economy now.

00:55:57.721 --> 00:56:00.657 position:63%
-We're gonna go into
the alphabet homes right now, right?

00:56:00.724 --> 00:56:02.292 position:63%
-For the scientists, for the workers.

00:56:02.359 --> 00:56:05.162 position:63%
For the scientists, from everybody,
blue collar, white collar.

00:56:05.229 --> 00:56:06.763 position:63%
-Okay. Let's check those out.

00:56:06.830 --> 00:56:08.538 position:63%
Yeah.
When you look at the different homes,

00:56:08.605 --> 00:56:09.871 position:63%
you can see who got what.

00:56:09.938 --> 00:56:14.471 position:63%
I mean the manager's house was amazing
and the workers get a prefab

00:56:14.538 --> 00:56:16.471 position:63%
that they stand up in one day

00:56:17.087 --> 00:56:18.608 position:63%
that's made out of two-by-twos.

00:56:18.675 --> 00:56:21.611 position:63%
And there's still
a bunch of those being lived in.

00:56:21.678 --> 00:56:24.314 position:63%
Oh, those homes were more than
a quarter million dollars.

00:56:24.381 --> 00:56:25.242 position:63%
It's outrageous.

00:56:25.309 --> 00:56:27.384 position:63%
I mean these aren't even
just temporary homes.

00:56:27.451 --> 00:56:30.187 position:63%
These were homes that were designed
by the Tennessee Valley Authority

00:56:30.254 --> 00:56:33.223 position:63%
in the New Deal
as like relief for farm workers.

00:56:33.290 --> 00:56:36.359 position:63%
Like, they weren't ever
meant to be permanent.

00:56:36.426 --> 00:56:38.562 position:63%
-You were there.
-Yeah, I was there.

00:56:38.629 --> 00:56:41.029 position:63%
-And you were there.
-You were there.

00:56:44.301 --> 00:56:48.805 position:63%
[Kirk] When the city was laid out,
they used military personnel's names

00:56:48.872 --> 00:56:50.974 position:63%
for most of these roads.

00:56:51.041 --> 00:56:54.444 position:63%
So this identifies who that is
for that street that you're…

00:56:54.511 --> 00:56:56.578 position:63%
-Colonel Harold Storrs Hetrick.

00:56:58.049 --> 00:57:00.917 position:63%
-Okay. So these were the homes.

00:57:00.984 --> 00:57:02.552 position:63%
[Kirk] Oh, we're in alphabet homes now.

00:57:02.619 --> 00:57:04.354 position:63%
-These are called alphabet homes.
-Yes.

00:57:04.421 --> 00:57:06.423 position:63%
-Built by the US government?
-Yes.

00:57:06.490 --> 00:57:08.558 position:63%
-It was upper management, right?
-Okay.

00:57:08.625 --> 00:57:10.065 position:63%
And you can see a lot of them,

00:57:10.132 --> 00:57:13.166 position:63%
they've done stuff to the facades
but these were all the same homes.

00:57:13.233 --> 00:57:17.367 position:63%
-So where we went today, I mean,
that drive to Hanford is what?

00:57:17.434 --> 00:57:20.804 position:63%
-45 minutes from here?
-Oh, yeah. But they were on a bus.

00:57:20.871 --> 00:57:24.541 position:63%
They built these homes. These were
privately owned homes here on this side.

00:57:24.608 --> 00:57:26.446 position:63%
But I built my house in '90.

00:57:27.077 --> 00:57:29.579 position:63%
-This is cool out here.
-We're a block from the river.

00:57:29.646 --> 00:57:31.314 position:63%
[Peter] When the government left town,

00:57:31.381 --> 00:57:33.850 position:63%
people could buy their home
that they lived in?

00:57:33.917 --> 00:57:36.353 position:63%
-Yes.
-How'd that work, Robert?

00:57:36.420 --> 00:57:40.357 position:63%
If it was a single-family home,
the person living in it could buy it

00:57:40.424 --> 00:57:42.125 position:63%
for the price government set.

00:57:42.192 --> 00:57:43.760 position:63%
If not it would go on the market.

00:57:43.827 --> 00:57:45.258 position:63%
If it was a duplex,

00:57:45.325 --> 00:57:49.271 position:63%
the person who had been living in
either side of the duplex the longest

00:57:49.338 --> 00:57:51.201 position:63%
could purchase the whole home.

00:57:51.268 --> 00:57:53.837 position:63%
And if they didn't purchase it,
then it went to the other person.

00:57:53.904 --> 00:57:55.805 position:63%
And if they didn't purchase it,
then it went up.

00:57:55.872 --> 00:57:57.640 position:63%
-Okay.
-Do you remember me, Kirk Christensen?

00:57:57.707 --> 00:57:59.575 position:63%
I was out there at the reactor
for quite a bit.

00:57:59.642 --> 00:58:02.345 position:63%
I knew you were familiar
but I couldn't place the name on you.

00:58:02.412 --> 00:58:04.981 position:63%
There you go.
Right. Yeah. Yeah, it's good to see you.

00:58:05.048 --> 00:58:07.150 position:63%
-Del, Peter Santenello.
-Your name again?

00:58:07.217 --> 00:58:09.185 position:63%
-Yeah. Peter Santenello.
-Peter, huh?

00:58:09.252 --> 00:58:10.453 position:63%
-Yeah. Nice to meet you, Del.

00:58:10.520 --> 00:58:13.657 position:63%
One of the biggest problems with me
is I can't hear.

00:58:13.724 --> 00:58:15.225 position:63%
We'll just talk louder then.

00:58:15.292 --> 00:58:17.260 position:63%
-You got a strong grip, Del.
-Oh my gosh.

00:58:17.327 --> 00:58:19.229 position:63%
-Let me get a hold of you.
-[laughter]

00:58:19.296 --> 00:58:21.898 position:63%
-Dude, Del is still in peak shape.
-Del is on fire.

00:58:21.965 --> 00:58:24.834 position:63%
-How old are you, Del?
-Three months, I'll be 97.

00:58:24.901 --> 00:58:26.269 position:63%
-Wow.
-Wow.

00:58:26.336 --> 00:58:28.638 position:63%
[Robert] Del's been here since 1950,
right, Del?

00:58:28.705 --> 00:58:29.640 position:63%
-51.
-51?

00:58:30.140 --> 00:58:31.675 position:63%
Were you working still, Chris?

00:58:31.742 --> 00:58:34.334 position:63%
No. Nope.
I retired nine years ago.

00:58:34.878 --> 00:58:37.514 position:63%
I've only been retired for 32 years.

00:58:37.581 --> 00:58:39.215 position:63%
So listen to this, Del.

00:58:39.282 --> 00:58:41.351 position:63%
We went to the Spudnut Shop this morning.

00:58:41.418 --> 00:58:42.686 position:63%
I took Peter in there.

00:58:42.753 --> 00:58:46.782 position:63%
It became a competition
to who retired the longest.

00:58:47.791 --> 00:58:49.735 position:63%
-And you win, Del.
-Yeah, you win.

00:58:49.802 --> 00:58:50.460 position:63%
Yeah.

00:58:50.527 --> 00:58:52.262 position:63%
[Peter] This is a great zone.

00:58:52.329 --> 00:58:55.598 position:63%
[Robert] Del was just out
doing yard work before we got here.

00:58:55.665 --> 00:58:58.501 position:63%
-So Del, when did you start
working out at the lab?

00:58:58.568 --> 00:59:01.938 position:63%
Well, the question,
I guess I'll transform a little bit.

00:59:02.005 --> 00:59:04.874 position:63%
I started, I came to Hanford in 1951.

00:59:04.941 --> 00:59:06.341 position:63%
Out of college.

00:59:06.843 --> 00:59:09.045 position:63%
And I went to work for GE.

00:59:09.112 --> 00:59:11.915 position:63%
GE, of course, managed the whole plant
and the city and everything.

00:59:11.982 --> 00:59:16.020 position:63%
They had a prime contract
with the Atomic Energy Commission.

00:59:16.087 --> 00:59:19.589 position:63%
I knew it was a government program
supporting the war effort.

00:59:19.656 --> 00:59:22.759 position:63%
And I, of course,
I lived during World War II.

00:59:22.826 --> 00:59:25.061 position:63%
I mean my family was deeply involved.

00:59:25.128 --> 00:59:29.566 position:63%
My two older brothers were in World War II
all the way through it.

00:59:30.333 --> 00:59:32.367 position:63%
And I was just a little too young.

00:59:32.434 --> 00:59:34.004 position:63%
So I knew what the war was all about.

00:59:34.071 --> 00:59:38.842 position:63%
I knew what the bomb, when the bomb
was dropped… bombs and atomic bombs.

00:59:38.909 --> 00:59:41.444 position:63%
And I knew it came,
some of the material came from Hanford.

00:59:41.511 --> 00:59:43.546 position:63%
But that's about all I knew.

00:59:43.613 --> 00:59:46.750 position:63%
I came here as a training,
as a rotational training.

00:59:46.817 --> 00:59:49.283 position:63%
But then I got into inspection for…

00:59:50.087 --> 00:59:52.689 position:63%
They were building
the C Reactor at that time.

00:59:52.756 --> 00:59:56.159 position:63%
For the Korean War,
they were starting to expand Hanford.

00:59:56.226 --> 01:00:00.363 position:63%
And the C Reactor, which is
right alongside of B.

01:00:00.430 --> 01:00:03.033 position:63%
And they were building
a lot of the material over in Seattle.

01:00:03.100 --> 01:00:06.269 position:63%
The shipyard had
the biological shield contract

01:00:06.336 --> 01:00:08.471 position:63%
to build this shield for the front wall.

01:00:08.538 --> 01:00:09.371 position:63%
Mm-hmm.

01:00:09.438 --> 01:00:12.142 position:63%
I went over there on inspection
with some of their people.

01:00:12.209 --> 01:00:13.643 position:63%
That was interesting work.

01:00:13.710 --> 01:00:17.047 position:63%
But living in Richland was a great place.

01:00:17.219 --> 01:00:23.820 position:63%
Of course I had never experienced any
mixing with society and the world, since…

01:00:23.887 --> 01:00:26.456 position:63%
you wouldn't even believe my background.

01:00:26.523 --> 01:00:30.795 position:63%
I want to go back. I was raised
on a dryland wheat farm in Montana.

01:00:30.862 --> 01:00:34.464 position:63%
Graduated from high school
and went to college at Montana State.

01:00:34.531 --> 01:00:39.402 position:63%
So coming out into society out here
was a major change within itself.

01:00:39.469 --> 01:00:42.572 position:63%
-This is quite remote,
but for you, it was more urban.

01:00:42.639 --> 01:00:45.308 position:63%
-There was more going on.
-For me it was a city, yeah.

01:00:45.375 --> 01:00:48.178 position:63%
Everybody had a job
and were committed to that job.

01:00:48.245 --> 01:00:52.415 position:63%
And of course, secrecy was the ultimate,
you know, during the Manhattan Project.

01:00:52.482 --> 01:00:56.419 position:63%
But even when I came,
things were pretty secret.

01:00:56.486 --> 01:00:59.389 position:63%
But of course,
people did talk about their jobs.

01:00:59.456 --> 01:01:03.226 position:63%
The students, you know,
the young bucks, the young engineers,

01:01:03.293 --> 01:01:07.598 position:63%
described always what they were,
what programs they were involved in.

01:01:07.665 --> 01:01:10.500 position:63%
Everybody was proud of the work
they were doing during the Cold War.

01:01:10.567 --> 01:01:14.225 position:63%
Of course increased production
and development of technology

01:01:14.292 --> 01:01:16.072 position:63%
was the thrust of the job.

01:01:16.139 --> 01:01:19.275 position:63%
And everybody was working
to that ultimate goal.

01:01:19.342 --> 01:01:22.479 position:63%
And I guess the other
was certainly a feeling of

01:01:22.546 --> 01:01:24.622 position:63%
camaraderie and support.

01:01:24.689 --> 01:01:26.149 position:63%
And…

01:01:26.216 --> 01:01:29.552 position:63%
-You were all in it together
to do something for the country?

01:01:29.619 --> 01:01:30.687 position:63%
The city, of course too.

01:01:30.754 --> 01:01:34.724 position:63%
And the city, everybody who lived
in the city was working at Hanford.

01:01:34.791 --> 01:01:37.494 position:63%
If you didn't work at Hanford,
you couldn't live in Richland.

01:01:37.561 --> 01:01:39.357 position:63%
Everybody in Richland was either employed

01:01:39.424 --> 01:01:42.528 position:63%
at the plant
or supporting services, you know,

01:01:42.595 --> 01:01:46.043 position:63%
local stores or shops or whatever.

01:01:46.570 --> 01:01:48.303 position:63%
But they were all employed

01:01:49.250 --> 01:01:51.422 position:63%
ultimately through the Hanford program.

01:01:51.942 --> 01:01:54.978 position:63%
-So Del, here's an interesting…
I just thought of this.

01:01:55.045 --> 01:01:58.378 position:63%
So in one way,
you're fighting the socialist world.

01:01:58.882 --> 01:02:02.614 position:63%
And then this town
is sort of a little microcosm

01:02:03.195 --> 01:02:04.528 position:63%
of socialist living.

01:02:05.522 --> 01:02:10.126 position:63%
Government housing, government job,
everything taken care of,

01:02:10.193 --> 01:02:12.460 position:63%
everyone sort of on the same page.

01:02:13.396 --> 01:02:16.699 position:63%
I'm just… It's an interesting irony,
is all I'm saying.

01:02:16.766 --> 01:02:19.669 position:63%
Yeah, and surprising there,
or maybe not surprising,

01:02:19.736 --> 01:02:23.206 position:63%
the people that lived in Pasco
and Kennewick thought those snobs

01:02:23.273 --> 01:02:25.993 position:63%
over there in Richland, you know,
they have everything given to them.

01:02:26.060 --> 01:02:27.663 position:63%
[laughter] -Right, right.

01:02:27.730 --> 01:02:29.014 position:63%
[Robert] It's true.

01:02:29.087 --> 01:02:31.047 position:63%
He's not wrong, yeah.

01:02:31.114 --> 01:02:33.349 position:63%
That was a real feeling
that people had, yeah.

01:02:33.416 --> 01:02:35.652 position:63%
We were very fortunate
in that respect, I guess.

01:02:35.719 --> 01:02:38.126 position:63%
But there was also a feeling of,

01:02:38.989 --> 01:02:42.058 position:63%
let's get the government
off our backs, you know too.

01:02:42.125 --> 01:02:45.929 position:63%
That ultimately developed,
when they started selling property,

01:02:45.996 --> 01:02:49.632 position:63%
I bought this lot as
some of the first property that was sold.

01:02:49.699 --> 01:02:51.901 position:63%
-The government owned the lot.
-Okay.

01:02:51.968 --> 01:02:55.004 position:63%
And I bought the lot on the first sale
that was made along the river,

01:02:55.071 --> 01:02:57.214 position:63%
and this was my third choice.

01:02:57.772 --> 01:02:59.742 position:63%
I tried to get one on the river,

01:02:59.809 --> 01:03:03.146 position:63%
but I didn't have the seniority
on the plant to do that.

01:03:03.213 --> 01:03:05.192 position:63%
They sold the lots based on

01:03:05.899 --> 01:03:08.238 position:63%
your age, your employment date,

01:03:08.997 --> 01:03:12.488 position:63%
what your seniority date was for Hanford,
working at Hanford.

01:03:12.555 --> 01:03:16.158 position:63%
And my wife had a longer date,

01:03:16.225 --> 01:03:18.258 position:63%
an earlier date than I did,

01:03:18.895 --> 01:03:21.881 position:63%
so we used her date to apply for a lot.

01:03:22.666 --> 01:03:24.534 position:63%
And we got our third choice.

01:03:24.601 --> 01:03:28.805 position:63%
-[Peter] This is beautiful.
-I had 10 fruit trees here at one time.

01:03:28.872 --> 01:03:31.056 position:63%
So what do you think about the town now?

01:03:31.123 --> 01:03:33.390 position:63%
It's more like society in general.

01:03:33.910 --> 01:03:36.346 position:63%
-How so?
-We didn't have any crime rate.

01:03:36.413 --> 01:03:39.046 position:63%
Early years, first 20, 30, 40 years,

01:03:39.113 --> 01:03:42.785 position:63%
the crime rate around Richland
was just zero, practically.

01:03:42.852 --> 01:03:47.590 position:63%
I mean it was a very safe place to live
and a very friendly place to live.

01:03:47.657 --> 01:03:51.991 position:63%
But over the years it's become
more like other cities, obviously,

01:03:52.729 --> 01:03:55.331 position:63%
because it is like other cities now.

01:03:55.398 --> 01:03:58.067 position:63%
-So Del, you were saying
your neighbor had a bomb shelter?

01:03:58.134 --> 01:03:59.269 position:63%
Yeah, in the front yard.

01:03:59.336 --> 01:04:00.364 position:63%
Spokane has a bunch.

01:04:00.431 --> 01:04:03.359 position:63%
Potential for atomic war was there!

01:04:03.426 --> 01:04:05.108 position:63%
-It was there.
-And we're a target.

01:04:05.175 --> 01:04:10.148 position:63%
I mean it's possible somebody'd
pull the trigger, I guess. I don't know.

01:04:13.450 --> 01:04:18.154 position:63%
[Del] Just passed away three years ago.
We were married for 69 and a half years.

01:04:18.221 --> 01:04:19.422 position:63%
[Peter] Wow.

01:04:19.489 --> 01:04:22.647 position:63%
What's the secret, Del,
to a long marriage like that?

01:04:22.714 --> 01:04:23.914 position:63%
That's impressive.

01:04:24.627 --> 01:04:26.658 position:63%
Dedication, respect,

01:04:27.720 --> 01:04:29.838 position:63%
cooperation, communication.

01:04:30.467 --> 01:04:33.066 position:63%
-Del, you're bald back then too, huh?

01:04:33.133 --> 01:04:34.296 position:63%
[laughter]

01:04:34.363 --> 01:04:37.496 position:63%
-In college they called me skinhead.
-Skinhead?

01:04:37.563 --> 01:04:38.396 position:63%
[laughter]

01:04:40.643 --> 01:04:42.412 position:63%
That's my parents there.

01:04:42.479 --> 01:04:43.312 position:63%
Up here?

01:04:43.379 --> 01:04:46.182 position:63%
My dad homesteaded in Montana in 1909.

01:04:46.249 --> 01:04:49.218 position:63%
-They have an intense look, both of them.
-[Kirk] Both of them.

01:04:49.285 --> 01:04:52.762 position:63%
The first William Ballard
came to America in 1632.

01:04:52.835 --> 01:04:53.824 position:63%
-Oh!
-Oh.

01:04:54.257 --> 01:04:56.793 position:63%
I've got records
of every Ballard back to then.

01:04:56.860 --> 01:04:59.529 position:63%
-Wow.
-That's my dad's homestead shack

01:04:59.596 --> 01:05:02.862 position:63%
back in Montana that he built in 1909.

01:05:03.600 --> 01:05:06.936 position:63%
-So you grew up out there?
-Here's where I grew up, right here.

01:05:08.338 --> 01:05:09.305 position:63%
Oh, wow.

01:05:09.372 --> 01:05:12.842 position:63%
My dad built that barn
essentially by himself.

01:05:12.909 --> 01:05:15.678 position:63%
It was the original store
out of Comanche, Montana.

01:05:15.745 --> 01:05:18.207 position:63%
He moved it over there,
jacked up the roof,

01:05:18.274 --> 01:05:21.165 position:63%
added the hip roofs, built the cow shed,

01:05:21.765 --> 01:05:23.264 position:63%
added the haymow floor,

01:05:23.331 --> 01:05:25.121 position:63%
and completed the building as a barn.

01:05:25.188 --> 01:05:27.513 position:63%
Essentially all by himself and horses.

01:05:27.580 --> 01:05:28.758 position:63%
No computers, no…

01:05:28.825 --> 01:05:32.895 position:63%
He didn't have electricity on this place
until I was in high school.

01:05:32.962 --> 01:05:36.029 position:63%
-Wow, Del. Grew up without electricity.
-Right.

01:05:36.566 --> 01:05:38.499 position:63%
-There's our telephone.
-Yeah.

01:05:38.955 --> 01:05:42.738 position:63%
[Kirk] How big was your farm, Del?
Your dad's farm?

01:05:42.805 --> 01:05:44.698 position:63%
When they homestead, it was 160 acres.

01:05:44.765 --> 01:05:48.221 position:63%
When I moved out,
he had about seven sections,

01:05:48.288 --> 01:05:51.781 position:63%
which is 640 acres per section.

01:05:51.848 --> 01:05:55.818 position:63%
Here from the New York Times,
that's me inside the FFTF.

01:05:55.885 --> 01:05:59.622 position:63%
-What does the FFTF stand for?
-Fast Flux Test Reactor.

01:05:59.689 --> 01:06:00.823 position:63%
I helped put it together.

01:06:00.890 --> 01:06:02.295 position:63%
I was the engineer inside

01:06:02.362 --> 01:06:05.361 position:63%
when we were putting
the components together in the reactor.

01:06:05.428 --> 01:06:09.298 position:63%
I was the engineer working
with Bechtel on the construction.

01:06:09.365 --> 01:06:14.737 position:63%
-What's the secret, Del, to 96?
Pepsi and working in nuclear reactors?

01:06:14.804 --> 01:06:18.141 position:63%
-A little radiation, it's good for you.
-[chuckling]

01:06:18.208 --> 01:06:19.475 position:63%
No, I never got much radiation.

01:06:19.542 --> 01:06:23.211 position:63%
Oh, I got some
but most of my jobs were construction.

01:06:23.278 --> 01:06:24.781 position:63%
So it's clean work.

01:06:24.848 --> 01:06:26.949 position:63%
-Yeah, a lot of mine was too.

01:06:27.016 --> 01:06:31.220 position:63%
-Did you see some people
that were affected by the radiation here?

01:06:31.287 --> 01:06:34.323 position:63%
There were, of course, the one accident,
which you're familiar with,

01:06:34.390 --> 01:06:37.160 position:63%
they had out there, that sodium or the…

01:06:37.227 --> 01:06:38.722 position:63%
-The McCluskey?
-McCluskey.

01:06:38.789 --> 01:06:40.263 position:63%
That was a serious situation.

01:06:40.330 --> 01:06:41.163 position:63%
-Yeah.

01:06:41.911 --> 01:06:45.501 position:63%
And of course, there were criticalities
where people had to evacuate.

01:06:45.568 --> 01:06:48.104 position:63%
But like, again, I said,
I was not in operations

01:06:48.171 --> 01:06:52.308 position:63%
and was very seldom around
any high radiation.

01:06:52.375 --> 01:06:54.575 position:63%
I had some projects that modified

01:06:55.278 --> 01:06:57.647 position:63%
some of the stuff that had been radiated,

01:06:57.714 --> 01:07:01.596 position:63%
but in the most part,
I worked in clean, new work.

01:07:15.398 --> 01:07:18.634 position:63%
[Peter] The SWAT vehicle's
